\textbf{Scalability Analysis:}
Fig. \ref{fig:scalability} demonstrates the performance of our proposed methods as the number of users in the environment increases. 
These results are collected from 1000 episodes of testing. 
In Fig. \ref{fig:scalability-a} and Fig. \ref{fig:scalability-b}, each bar shows the average task response time and energy consumption, respectively. The vertical lines represent the standard deviation of the response time and the energy consumption.
The response time and energy consumption are expected to grow as the the number of users in the environment increases since the MEC's computational resources is shared among all users.
However, we see that CPPG performance does not vary significantly as the number of users increases.
This is due to the fact that CPPG is a centralized decision making agent and has full observability of the environment, thus can capture the communication computation trade-offs in a multi-agent setting.
On the contrary, the other methods are decentralized and each agent makes an independent decision based on its partial observation of the state of the environment, thus each agent makes a sub-optimal decision.
