\subsection*{Effects of contribution adjustments on achieving full cooperation}

In the above, we consider the scenario where the contribution matrix remains constant across in homogeneous hypergraphs. We now extend this investigation to assess the impact of changes in the contribution matrix on the feasible intervals of full cooperation.

We explore a homogeneous partially connected 6-node hypergraph with a hyperdegree of 2 (Fig. \ref{fig3}\textbf{a}). In the previous subsection, we assume that all players contribute equally to each participating game. Here, we modify this setup by adjusting contributions such that each player donates a proportion of \( p \) to one side and \( 1 - p \) to the other. For example, player 1 contributes \( p \) to hyperedge \( h_1 \), and similarly, players 2 and 3 contribute \( p \) to hyperedge \( h_2 \), player 4 to hyperedge \( h_3 \), and players 5 and 6 to hyperedge \( h_4 \) also contribute \( p \). Varying \( p \) from 0.5 to 1 demonstrates that increasing bias does not favor cooperation (Fig. \ref{fig3}\textbf{a}). Further experiments, including adjusting contributions for players 1, 3, and 5 to one side and players 2, 4, and 6 to the opposite side, reveal no enhancement in the proportion of fully cooperative feasible intervals (Fig. \ref{fig3}\textbf{c}). We also explore various scenarios, including contribution changes for one player, two players, and arbitrary contribution changes for all players (Extended Data \ref{supplementary_figure3}). Regardless of how the direction of contributions is altered, equal contributions are found to be the most effective for promoting cooperation in homogeneous hypergraphs. Moreover, we isolated the scenario where \( p = 0.8 \) (Fig. \ref{fig3}\textbf{b} and \textbf{d}) and observed that the \(\delta\) corresponding to the optimized endowments is lower than the \(\delta_{\mathrm{equal}}^{*}\) corresponding to equal endowments. Therefore, when contributions are unequal, unequal endowments more effectively promote cooperation.

In the above study, we fixed a contribution proportion \( p \) and determined productivity, investigating the proportion of the corresponding endowment space that allows for full cooperation. Below, we discuss the scenario where a fixed endowment distribution and players' productivity are given, examining which contribution strategies among all possible ones are most conducive to promoting cooperation. We continue to use a 6-node homogeneous partially connected hypergraph as our study subject (Fig. \ref{fig4}\textbf{a}).




