\subsubsection{Ablation Study}

To comprehensively evaluate the contributions of each component in the proposed AeroGPT framework, we conducted an ablation study. \autoref{tab:ablation} presents the performance metrics when including different combinations of the three primary components: Foundation Model (FM), which represents the model which has gone through Audio Knowledge Acquisition process, with general audio understanding capabilities; Vibration Signal Alignment (VSA), which bridges the domain gap between general audio and aero-engine bearing signal domain; and Generative Fault Classification (GFC), which employs fine-tuning techniques for direct fault label generation. All experiments maintained identical testing conditions with the same dataset split and evaluation metrics to ensure fair comparisons.

The results reveal that utilizing only the Foundation Model (FM) without any adaptation yields poor performance, with merely 14.87\% accuracy, 6.31\% precision, and 4.20\% F1-score, which is approximately equivalent to random guessing. This underscores the significant domain gap between general audio understanding and aerospace bearing vibration analysis. When incorporating the VSA component while omitting GFC, a modest improvement can be observed in performance—accuracy increases by 5.78\% to 20.65\%, precision nearly doubles to 12.47\%, and F1-score improves by 6.63\% to 10.83\%. This enhancement, while limited, indicates that the VSA stage establishes preliminary connections between general audio knowledge and vibration-specific patterns, though insufficient for accurate fault classification in isolation.

Notably, integrating GFC directly with the FM without the VSA intermediary yields a dramatic performance leap. The FM+GFC configuration achieves 97.21\% accuracy, 97.84\% precision and 97.52\% F1-score, representing absolute improvements of 82.34\%, 91.53\%, and 93.32\% in the respective metrics compared to using FM alone. This substantial enhancement demonstrates that PEFT enables effective adaptation of the foundation model to the target task, even without explicit domain alignment. The generative approach to fault classification proves highly effective in capturing the discriminative patterns in aerospace bearing vibration signals.

The complete AeroGPT framework, incorporating all three components (FM+VSA+GFC), achieves the highest performance across all metrics: 98.94\% accuracy, 99.16\% precision, and 99.02\% F1-score. Compared to the FM+GFC configuration, the full framework yields further absolute improvements of 1.73\%, 1.32\%, and 1.50\% in accuracy, precision, and F1-score, respectively. This corresponds to relative error reductions of 62.1\% in misclassification rate, 61.1\% in precision errors, and 60.5\% in F1-score deficiency. These results confirm the synergistic effect of the VSA stage, which, while providing minimal benefits in isolation, significantly enhances the effectiveness of the subsequent GFC stage by aligning the foundation model's representations with the aerospace bearing vibration signal domain.

The significant gap between configurations with and without GFC highlights the critical role of task-specific fine-tuning in adapting general-purpose models to specialized domains like aerospace bearing fault diagnosis. However, the performance improvement from adding VSA to GFC demonstrates that bridging the domain gap through intermediate pre-training remains valuable, especially for highly specialized technical applications such as aerospace propulsion systems monitoring.

