To comprehensively evaluate the efficacy of the proposed AeroGPT framework, we conducted comparative experiments against a diverse range of advanced deep learning architectures representing the current state-of-the-art in deep-learning-based classification tasks. The benchmark models span multiple architectural paradigms, including: MA1DCNN \cite{wang2020understanding}, a specialized multi-scale attention-enhanced convolutional architecture optimized for bearing vibration analysis; ResNet50 \cite{he2016deep}, a deep residual network that mitigates gradient vanishing through skip connections; ConvNeXt V2 \cite{woo_convnext_2023}, a modern convolutional design incorporating depthwise convolutions and layer normalization; Conv-Transformer, a hybrid architecture leveraging both local convolutional feature extraction and global attention mechanisms; Masked AutoEncoder \cite{he_masked_2021}, a self-supervised learning approach that reconstructs masked input segments; PoolFormer \cite{yu_metaformer_2022}, which replaces attention mechanisms with simpler pooling operations while maintaining competitive performance; and the hierarchical Swin-Transformer architectures (original \cite{liu_swin_2021} and V2 \cite{liu_swin_2022}), which compute self-attention within shifted windows to achieve linear computational complexity with respect to image size. For vision-based architectures like ResNet and Swin-Transformer, vibration signals were converted to two-dimensional feature maps before processing.

As evidenced in \autoref{tab:comparison}, AeroGPT achieves substantial performance advantages across nearly all evaluation metrics, with particularly pronounced improvements in defective state recognition. The overall accuracy of 98.94\% represents a 2.73\% improvement over the next best performer (MA1DCNN at 96.21\%) and more than 8 percentage points above most transformer-based architectures. This performance gap underscores a fundamental limitation in conventional approaches: despite their sophisticated architectures, models designed for generic feature extraction struggle to capture the patterns of bearing faults without domain-specific adaptation.

A distinctive pattern emerges when examining performance across defective and non-defective categories. Traditional architectures exhibit a substantial performance asymmetry, with accuracy discrepancies exceeding 20 percentage points in several cases (e.g., ConvNeXt V2: 95.55\% vs. 72.05\%; Conv-Transformer: 96.11\% vs. 71.16\%; PoolFormer: 94.79\% vs. 67.19\%). This indicates a fundamental challenge in discriminating between different fault types compared to distinguishing between healthy and faulty states. In contrast, AeroGPT demonstrates remarkable category balance, with comparable performance across both non-defective (99.46\%) and defective (98.42\%) classifications. This balanced capability is particularly critical in aerospace applications, where missed defects can lead to catastrophic failures and false alarms result in unnecessary and costly maintenance interventions.

The performance advantage of AeroGPT stems from its architectural advantages. First, unlike conventional models that build representations from scratch, AeroGPT leverages and adapts the rich acoustic domain knowledge, which provides a sophisticated basis for interpreting mechanical vibration patterns that share fundamental properties with acoustic phenomena. Second, the VSA stage effectively bridges the domain gap between general audio and aerospace-specific vibration patterns, as confirmed by the ablation study results.

Notably, even the most advanced transformer-based architectures like Swin-Transformer V2, which achieve state-of-the-art performance in many computer vision tasks, fall significantly short in this domain-specific application. This emphasizes that architectural sophistication alone is insufficient for specialized engineering tasks—domain knowledge integration.

\subsubsection{Qualitative Analysis}

\begin{figure*}[h]
    \centering
\includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{qualitative_case1.pdf}
    \caption{Examples of AeroGPT's generative fault diagnosis capability and answers to follow-up queries.}
    \label{fig:qualitative_case1}
\end{figure*}

Beyond quantitative metrics, AeroGPT's interactive diagnostic capabilities were evaluated to assess practical utility in aerospace maintenance scenarios. \autoref{fig:qualitative_case1} demonstrates how AeroGPT leverages its generative foundation to transform fault diagnosis from isolated classification to interactive consultation, enabling multifaceted analysis of bearing defects.

Instead of producing a simple fault label, AeroGPT generates a detailed fault description when initially queried about defect presence. The framework's output explicitly identifies the fault as ``inner ring indentation'' with a severity of ``150 mm,'' providing immediate context for maintenance personnel. When subsequently questioned about severity, the system demonstrates nuanced assessment capabilities, classifying the defect as ``moderate'' while noting it ``should be monitored closely to prevent further deterioration.'' This level of granular evaluation is not typically available in traditional classification outputs, which often lack interpretability and actionable insights.

Moreover, AeroGPT exhibits causal reasoning by establishing explicit connections between vibration patterns and their mechanical implications. When asked about performance impacts, it articulates how inner ring indentation directly causes ``increased vibration'' and reduced ``efficiency and lifespan'' of the bearing component. This mechanical insight contrasts sharply with traditional classifiers that provide no explanation of fault mechanisms or consequences, merely indicating fault presence and category.

The framework further demonstrates domain expertise through contextually appropriate technical recommendations. When queried about inspection methodologies, AeroGPT recommends specific diagnostic tools, ``a borescope for visual inspection and an ultrasonic device to measure the depth of the indentation'', which are tailored to the identified fault type. Similarly, when asked about factors that might exacerbate the condition, the system identifies ``high-speed rotation, heavy loads, or high temperatures'' as operational parameters that could accelerate deterioration, providing valuable risk management insights for maintenance scheduling.

AeroGPT's ability to generate contextually appropriate maintenance recommendations calibrated to aerospace standards represents perhaps its most significant operational advantage. The response prescribing a ``maintenance check to inspect the bearing and potentially replace the inner ring'' aligns with established protocols for aerospace bearing defects of this category and severity. Furthermore, AeroGPT can incorporate domain-specific contextual information from technical maintenance manuals as additional input, enabling fine-grained recommendation calibration that precisely adheres to manufacturer specifications or organizational maintenance procedures, further enhancing recommendation precision. This multifaceted capability effectively bridges the critical gap between fault detection and maintenance action that typically requires expert human interpretation of classification outputs.

These examples confirm that AeroGPT's generative approach fundamentally transforms fault diagnosis methodology through a comprehensive set of capabilities spanning detection, assessment, causal analysis, technical guidance, risk evaluation, and intervention planning—all delivered through natural language interaction. The system's ability to reason about mechanical implications and prescribe appropriate interventions represents a paradigm shift in the interaction between maintenance personnel and diagnostic systems in safety-critical aerospace applications. By providing this complete diagnostic workflow within a conversational interface, AeroGPT significantly reduces the expertise barrier for maintenance personnel, potentially accelerating response times and improving maintenance outcomes in aerospace operations.

\subsection{Case 2: HIT Bearing Dataset}
\subsubsection{Dataset Descrption}

The second dataset utilized was obtained by Hou \textit{et al.} from the Harbin Institute of Technology (HIT) \cite{hou2023intershaft}, featuring inter-shaft bearing data from an aero-engine system. Their experimental setup comprises a modified aero-engine with the critical dual-rotor structure preserved. The test rig follows a similar structure with that in the first case, with the low-pressure rotor connected to the high-pressure rotor through a shaft and the inter-shaft bearings subjecting to various fault conditions. The system is equipped with two independent motors for the high and low pressure rotors, allowing for precise control over operational parameters. All signals were sampled at 25kHz, with tests conducted across 28 distinct operating conditions varying both speed (ranging from 1000 r/min to 6000 r/min) and speed ratio between the rotors (1.2 to 1.8). The dataset encompasses 2412 sets of vibration data labeled into three categories (healthy, inner ring defect, and outer ring defect), segmented into 20480-point sequences. The obtained training set consists of 1929 samples and the test set contains 483 samples.

