\subsubsection{Qualitative Analysis}

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{qualitative_case2.pdf}
    \caption{Comparison of AeroGPT's generative fault diagnosis ability with general-purpose models and conventional approaches.}
    \label{fig:qualitative_case2}
\end{figure*}

The comparative qualitative evaluation illustrated in \autoref{fig:qualitative_case2} demonstrates AeroGPT's distinctive advantages over both general-purpose large language models and traditional deep learning approaches when diagnosing aero-engine bearing faults.

When presented with identical vibration signals, AeroGPT produces a definitive diagnosis with high specificity, stating unequivocally ``Yes, there is an indentation on the inner ring.'' This precise identification contrasts sharply with the responses from other large language models, which exhibit significant diagnostic uncertainty despite similar architectural capabilities. The large foundation audio model, Qwen2-Audio-Instruct, produces the tentative assessment ``Yes, the signal indicates that there may be a defect,'' while the general-purpose model ChatGPT similarly suggests `Yes, the yellow part indicates that there may be a defect in the bearing.'' The hedging language reflects these models' lack of specialized knowledge alignment with bearing vibration patterns, resulting in diagnostically ambiguous conclusions that offer limited practical utility for maintenance personnel.

The diagnostic specificity gap becomes even more pronounced when users request detailed fault information. When asked to distinguish between inner and outer ring defects, AeroGPT maintains its diagnostic precision, confidently identifying the inner ring location. In contrast, both Qwen2-Audio and ChatGPT acknowledge a fundamental limitation with the response ``There is no sufficient information to determine.'' This inability to provide location-specific diagnosis represents a significant practical limitation, as repair protocols and maintenance prioritization depend heavily on defect location within the bearing assembly.

Traditional deep learning methods present an entirely different set of limitations. While potentially achieving comparable classification accuracy, these approaches output numerical probability distributions (e.g., [0.13, 0.82, 0.05]) that require additional post-processing and expert interpretation to translate into actionable diagnoses. The necessity of this translation layer introduces potential inconsistency and significantly limits practical utility in maintenance contexts. The workflow typically involves programming and training a model, obtaining a probability distribution across classes, and then performing post-processing to map the highest probability (0.82 in this case) to a predefined class label (Class 1). Even after this multi-step process, the system remains inherently non-interactive, with the notation ``unable to interact with'' highlighting a fundamental limitation—these systems cannot engage in follow-up diagnostic discussions or provide explanations for their classifications.

AeroGPT eliminates these limitations through its knowledge-aligned generative approach. By directly outputting precise fault characterizations in natural language and supporting location-specific diagnoses without additional post-processing, it bridges the interpretability gap that typically separates diagnostic systems from maintenance personnel. This capability transforms the diagnostic paradigm from a complex multi-stage analysis requiring specialized expertise to an intuitive conversational process accessible to broader maintenance staff. The directness and specificity of AeroGPT's diagnostic outputs represent a significant advancement in practical utility for aerospace applications, where maintenance decisions must be made promptly and with high confidence to ensure operational safety.

\section{Conclusion}
This paper proposed AeroGPT, a novel framework based on large-scale audio model that transfers knowledge from general audio domains to aero-engine bearing fault diagnosis. By recognizing the intrinsic acoustic-like nature of bearing vibration signals, AeroGPT addresses fundamental limitations in current aero-engine fault diagnosis approaches. The framework's two key innovations, Vibration Signal Alignment (VSA) and Generative Fault Classification (GFC), provide a systematic methodology for adapting general audio knowledge to domain-specific vibration patterns while enabling direct generation of interpretable diagnostic outputs. Through comprehensive experimental validation on two aerospace bearing datasets, AeroGPT achieved exceptional performance with 98.94\% accuracy on the DIRG dataset and perfect 100\% classification on the HIT bearing dataset, surpassing traditional deep learning approaches. The qualitative analysis further demonstrated AeroGPT's unique capability to provide definitive, specific fault characterizations in natural language, contrasting sharply with the uncertainty exhibited by general-purpose language models and the post-processing requirements of conventional classification approaches. This transformation of fault diagnosis from complex analysis to intuitive conversation significantly enhances practical utility in aerospace maintenance contexts, where rapid, accurate interpretation is essential for preventing catastrophic failures. By eliminating the need for post-processing and providing interactive, interpretable, and actionable diagnostics, AeroGPT represents a significant advancement in aerospace reliability engineering, highlighting the substantial potential of large-scale audio models to revolutionize fault diagnosis in industrial settings.

