The other parameters have slightly different definitions depending on the method. The number of edges is set at 1, 2 or 5. For Barab\'{a}si-Albert, this represents the number of nodes to which each new node connects, resulting in a large difference in degree across the nodes. For Watts–Strogatz, on the other hand, this is half the initial degree that all the nodes have before some of the edges are rewired. 

The edge (rewiring) probability is set to 0.001 and 0.01. The Erd\H{o}s-R\'{e}nyi method uses this parameter to express the probability of having an edge between any two nodes. The Watts–Strogatz method starts with a fixed number of nodes, and uses this parameter to express the probability that an edge changes its end node (rewiring). 

Smurfing patterns are injected after network generation. The three types of smurfing patterns that we consider in the experiments, and which are visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:injection}, are the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Separate:} The smurfing patterns are not connected to the rest of the network; 
\item \textbf{External smurfs:} Mules are added between two existing entities in the network; 
\item \textbf{Internal smurfs:} Existing nodes in the network are assigned the roles of sender, receiver and mule, and corresponding transactions are added.  
\end{enumerate}

