\section{Inter-reducibility of Weak and Full Opacity}\label{section:tools}
%
%

In this section, we prove a new result relating weak and full opacity (\cref{ss:weak=full}).
%
To this end, we first introduce in \cref{section:tools:Apriv-Apub} a construction---that will also be useful to prove our subsequent results in \cref{section:opacity:TA:res,section:finite}.





%
\subsection{$\APriv$ and $\APub$}\label{section:tools:Apriv-Apub}
%

%

First, we need a construction of two TAs $\APriv$ and $\APub$ that recognize timed words produced respectively by private and public runs of a given TA~$\TA$.


The public runs TA $\APub$ is the easiest to build: it suffices to remove the private locations from~$\TA$ to eliminate every private run in the system.
(See formal definition in \HALVersion{\cref{definition:APub} in \cref{appendix:definitions}}\ICFEMVersion{Definition~11 in \cite[Appendix~A]{ADL24report}}.)
%

The private runs TA $\APriv$ is obtained by duplicating all locations and transitions of~$\TA$: one copy $\TA_{S}$ corresponds to the paths that already visited the private locations set, and the other copy $\TA_{\bar{S}}$ corresponds to the paths that did not (this is a usual way to encode a Boolean, here ``$\PrivSet$ was visited'', in the locations of a TA).
For each private location $\locpriv$ in $\TA$ we copy all transitions leading to the copy of
$\locpriv$ in $\TA_{\bar{S}}$ and redirect them to the copy of $\locpriv$ in $\TA_{S}$. The initial location is the one from $\TA_{\bar{S}}$ and the final locations are the ones from~$\TA_{S}$.
Hence all runs need to go from $\TA_{\bar{S}}$ to $\TA_{S}$ before reaching a final location, which requires visiting a private location.
%
%
%

\begin{definition}[Private runs TA $\APriv$]

Let $\TA = \TAprivextend$ be a TA.
	The private runs TA \mbox{$\APriv = (\ActionSet, \LocSet_S \uplus \LocSet_{\bar{S}}, \loci{0}^{\bar{S}}, \PrivSet^S, \FinalSet^S, \ClockSet, \invariant', \EdgeSet')$} is defined as follows:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\LocSet_S  =  \lbrace \loc^S \mid \loc \in \LocSet \rbrace$ and $\LocSet_{\bar{S}}  =   \lbrace \loc^{\bar{S}} \mid \loc \in \LocSet \rbrace$.
		\LongVersion{%
		That is,
			$\LocSet_S$ and $\LocSet_{\bar{S}}$ are two disjoint copies of $\LocSet$: each location $\loc$ of $\LocSet$ corresponds to a location denoted $\loc^S$ in $\LocSet_S$ and a location $\loc^{\bar{S}}$ in $\LocSet_{\bar{S}}$ (for instance $\loci{0}^{\bar{S}}$ is in $\LocSet_{\bar{S}}$)
		}

		\item $\FinalSet^S = \lbrace \locfinal^S \mid \locfinal \in \FinalSet \rbrace$ is the set of final locations, and $\PrivSet^S= \lbrace \locpriv^S \mid \locpriv \in \PrivSet \rbrace$ is the set of private locations;

%
%
%
%
		\item $\invariant'$ is defined such as $\invariant'(\loc^S) = \invariant'(\loc^{\bar{S}}) = \invariant(\loc)$

		\item $\EdgeSet' = \EdgeSet_S \uplus \EdgeSet_{\bar{S}} \uplus \EdgeSet_{\bar{S} \rightarrow S}$ where $\EdgeSet_S$ and $\EdgeSet_{\bar{S}}$ are the two disjoint copies of $\EdgeSet$ respectively associated with the sets of locations $\LocSet_S$ and $\LocSet_{\bar{S}}$, and $\EdgeSet_{\bar{S} \rightarrow S}$ is a copy of the set of all transitions that go toward $\PrivSet^{\bar{S}}$ where the target location $\locpriv^{\bar{S}}$ has been changed into $\locpriv^{S}$.
		More formally:
		\[\begin{array}{rcl}
		\EdgeSet_S & = & \big\lbrace (\loc^S, g, a, R, \loc'^S) \mid (\loc, g, a, R, \loc') \in \EdgeSet \big\rbrace \\
		\EdgeSet_{\bar{S}} & = &  \big\lbrace (\loc^{\bar{S}}, g, a, R, \loc'^{\bar{S}}) \mid (\loc, g, a, R, \loc') \in \EdgeSet \big\rbrace \\
		\EdgeSet_{\bar{S} \rightarrow S} & = & \big\lbrace (\loc^{\bar{S}}, g, a, R, \locpriv^S) \mid (\loc, g, a, R, \locpriv) \in \EdgeSet \big\rbrace\text{.}
		\end{array}\]
	\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}


