%
\section{Opacity Problems for Subclasses of Timed Automata}\label{section:opacity:TA:res}
%
%

%
%
In this section, we consider the decidability status and complexities of the three opacity problems presented in \cref{section:opacity:TA} for several subclasses of TAs: TAs with one clock, TAs with one action, TAs under discrete time and observable ERAs.
%
We first show the decidability of the $\exists$-opacity problem in the general case.
Then, we focus on each class of TAs listed above to study weak and full opacity.



%
\subsection{$\exists$-opacity Problem}\label{section:opacity:exists}
%

%
%
%
%
%
%
We show here (see \HALVersion{\cref{appendix:exist}}\ICFEMVersion{\cite[Appendix~B]{ADL24report}}) that in general the $\exists$-opacity problem is \PSPACE-complete relying on the reachability problem in TAs, which is known to be
\PSPACE-complete~\cite{AD94} as well, even for TAs with two clocks~\cite{FJ15}.
This theorem considers multiple subclasses of TAs which we will describe more in depth in future sections.


%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:decidability-exists-opacity}
Given a TA $\TA$, 
deciding the $\exists$-opacity problem for $\TA$ is \PSPACE-complete, even when restricting
$\TA$ to be a one-action TA, discrete-time TA, an oERA\footnote{See~\cref{section:opacity:era}.}, or a single clock TA where integers appearing in guards are given in binary.

If the number of clocks in $\TA$ is fixed and integers appearing in guards are given in unary, the $\exists$-opacity problem is in \NLOGSPACE.
\end{theorem}





%
\subsection{Timed Automata with a Single Action}\label{section:opacity:one-action}
%



Recall that the universality problem consists in deciding whether a TA~$\TA$ accepts the set of all timed words.
In~\cite{OW04}, it is shown that the class of one-action~TAs is one of the simplest cases for which the universality problem is undecidable among TAs. 
Therefore, this gives the intuition (see \HALVersion{\cref{app:one-action}}\ICFEMVersion{\cite[Appendix~C]{ADL24report}} for proof) that the weak and full opacity problems are undecidable as well for one-action~TAs ($\vert \ActionSet \vert = 1$).

%
%
%
%
%

%
%
%
%
%

%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{th:undecidability:opacity:one-action}
	The full and weak opacity problems for TAs with one action are undecidable.
\end{theorem}
%

%

%
%
%
%




%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%

%
\begin{remark}
The problems of execution-time opacity introduced in~\cite{ALLMS23} are a particular \emph{decidable} subcase of these undecidable opacity problems with one-action~TAs.
Indeed, the execution time is equivalent to a \emph{unique} timestamp associated with the last action of the system.
\end{remark}
%


%
\subsection{Timed Automata with a Single Clock}\label{section:opacity:one-clock}
%

Following the same reasoning as in \cref{section:opacity:one-action} (based on a different existing result on TAs), we show that full opacity is undecidable for one-clock TAs.

%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{th:undecidability:full-opacity:one-clock-epsilon}
	The full and weak opacity problems for one-clock TAs are undecidable.
\end{theorem}
%
\begin{proof}
	By reusing the same proof argument as in \cref{th:undecidability:opacity:one-action}, using the fact that universality for one-clock TAs (with $\varepsilon$-transitions) is undecidable~\cite{ADOQW08}.
\end{proof}
%

%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%



%
\subsubsection{Without $\silentaction$-transitions}
%



We now prove that the weak and full opacity problems become both decidable in the context of one-clock~TAs ($\vert \ClockSet \vert = 1$) \emph{without} $\silentaction$-transitions, relying on the fact that the language inclusion problem for one-clock~TAs without $\silentaction$-transitions is decidable~\cite{OW04}.

%
%
%
%
%
%
%

By definition, a TA is weakly opaque if $\PrivateTr{\TA}$ is included in $\PublicTr{\TA}$.
As $\PrivateTr{\TA}$ and $\PublicTr{\TA}$ are respectively recognized by $\APriv$ and 
$\APub$, the decidability of the weak opacity problem is directly obtained from the
decidability of the inclusion of two languages. 
Full opacity follows immediately, from the bidirectional language inclusion.
%

%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:one-clock-noepsilon}
Full and weak opacity are decidable for one-clock~TAs without $\silentaction$-transitions.
\end{theorem}
%

Note however that, while decidable, this problem cannot be effectively solved as
the algorithm given by~\cite{OW04} is non-primitive recursive. 
Moreover, this bound is tight as shown in~\cite{ADOQW08}. 
Hence, by imitating the approach of~\cref{th:undecidability:opacity:one-action}\LongVersion{, replacing location $\loc_{\star}$ by a copy of the second automaton}, one can reduce the language inclusion problem to the weak opacity, and thus show the complexity is tight for weak and full opacity as well.


%
\begin{remark}\label{remark:noepsilon:nocontradiction}
	This result might seem to contradict the result of a concurrently written paper~\cite{AGWZH24} that proves undecidability of (language-based) opacity for one-clock TAs without $\silentaction$-transitions---but it does not.
	The discrepancy comes from the fact that our attacker observes all actions (the unobservable actions are encoded into $\silentaction$-transitions), while their setting considers unobservable actions---which can act as $\silentaction$-transitions even in the absence of syntactic $\silentaction$-transitions.
\end{remark}
%

Now, due to the undecidability of language universality for TAs with at least two clocks~\cite[Theorem~21]{OW04}, we can prove the following with the same construction as in \cref{th:undecidability:opacity:one-action}:

%
%
\begin{theorem}\label{th:undecidability:full-opacity:two-clocks}
	Full and weak opacity are undecidable for TAs with $\geq 2$ clocks.
\end{theorem}
%









%
%
%
%

%
%
%
%
%
%
%
%


%

%
%
%

%
\subsection{Timed Automata over Discrete Time}\label{section:opacity:discrete-time}
%

In the general case, clocks are real-valued variables, with valuations thus ranging over
$\Time = \setRgeqzero$.
TAs over discrete time however restrict the clock's behavior to valuations over $\Time = \setN$.
Since the arguments used in~\cite{AD94} to prove the undecidability of the universality problem in TAs rely on continuous time, this proof cannot be used to establish undecidability of opacity over discrete time.
In fact, relying on the region automaton (defined in \cref{section:tools:RA}) over discrete time and classical results on finite regular automata, we show \emph{decidability} of the opacity problems as well as their exact complexity.%

%

%

If $\mu$, $\mu'$ are two discrete clock valuations (\ie{} with values in $\setN$), the definition of $\simeq$ from \cref{section:tools:RA} can be simplified into: $\mu \simeq \mu'$ if and only if for each $\clock \in \ClockSet$, either $\mu(\clock) = \mu'(\clock)$ or $\mu (\clock) > M(\clock)$ and $\mu' (\clock) > M(\clock)$.

In continuous time, for each run of the TA, there is a unique corresponding run of the region automaton.
In discrete time, thanks to the simplified form of the definition of~$\simeq$, the converse statement that a run of the region automaton corresponds to a unique run of the TA nearly holds.
Loss of information however remains when every clock goes beyond their maximum constant, as time elapsing is not measured beyond this point.
In order to measure it, we add a letter~$t$ (for ticks) which occurs each time that an (integral) time unit passes in the region automaton.
This change can be operated directly on the TA~$\TA$ so that the correspondence between paths of~$\TA$ and $\RegionAutomaton{\TA}$ becomes immediate.

More precisely, we add a clock~$z$ and add self-loop transitions $e_t = (\loc, (z=1), t, \lbrace z \rbrace, \loc)$ on each location $\loc \in \LocSet$ of~$\TA$.
We also add the guard~``$z=0$'' to each discrete transition of~$\TA$.

We illustrate the resulting TA on a simple example in \cref{figure:example-RA}.
