\subsection{Observable Event-Recording Automata}\label{section:opacity:era}
%
%
In~\cite{Cassez09}, the opacity problems were shown to be undecidable for Event-Recording Automata (ERAs)~\cite{AFH99}, a subclass of TAs where every clock $\clock$ is associated with a specific event $a_x$ and $x$ is reset on a transition if and only if this transition is labeled by~$a_x$.
Due to this, the valuations of clocks are entirely determined by the duration since the last occurrence of the associated events.
One of the main interest of ERAs is that they are determinizable~\cite{AFH99}.
This determinization is carried out through the standard subset construction.
%
%
%

The undecidability result from~\cite{Cassez09} on ERAs required to make the events $a_x$ unobservable.
Hence, in our framework they would be replaced by $\silentaction$-transitions.
We define observable ERAs (oERAs) as ERAs where the actions resetting the clocks must be observable.
This means that the information required for the determinization now belongs to the trace that is observed.


Given an oERA~$\TA$, we can thus build through the subset construction a TA $\mathit{Det}_\TA$ such that any path $\rho$ in
$\TA$ corresponds to a path $\rho_D$ in $\mathit{Det}_\TA$ with the same trace and ending in a location labeled by the set of all the locations of $\TA$ that can be reached with a run that has the same trace as~$\rho$.
This information, combined with the construction of~$\AMemo$ (\cref{remark:APub-APriv:AMemo}) which stores in the state of the TA whether the  private location was visited or not, provides the following result (see \HALVersion{\cref{app:oera}}\ICFEMVersion{\cite[Appendix~E]{ADL24report}}).

%
\begin{theorem}\label{th:decidability:oera}
Both weak and full opacity are \PSPACE-complete for oERAs.
\end{theorem}











