 \section{Complexity of the $\exists$-opacity Problem }\label{appendix:exist}
%
%



\subsection{$\exists$-opacity Problem for General TAs}


Let us first show that the $\exists$-opacity problem for TA lies in \PSPACE.

\begin{proof}
Let $\TA$ be a~TA.
%
We build $\APriv$ and $\APub$ from $\TA$ as described in \cref{section:tools:Apriv-Apub}.
Noting that the product of two TAs recognizes the intersection of their languages~\cite[Theorem~3.15]{AD94} (assuming the two TAs share no clock), we build
the TA $\APriv \times \APub$, product of $\APriv$ and $\APub$, 
which language is $\PrivateTr{\TA} \cap \PublicTr{\TA}$. 
To build this product, we can rename all clocks from $\APub$ so that $\APriv$ and $\APub$ share no clock.


The $\exists$-opacity problem is by definition the non-emptiness of the intersection of $\PrivateTr{\TA}$ and $\PublicTr{\TA}$. 
Moreover, the reachability of a final location of $\APriv \times \APub$ is equivalent to the non-emptiness of the language of $\APriv \times \APub$, and thus of the set
$\PrivateTr{\TA} \cap \PublicTr{\TA}$.
Since reachability is decidable in \PSPACE in TAs~\cite{AD94}, the same holds for the 
$\exists$-opacity problem.\qed
\end{proof}


We now reduce the reachability problem for timed automata, known to be \PSPACE-complete, to the $\exists$-opacity problem.


\begin{proof}
Let $\TA = (\ActionSet, L, \locinit, \emptyset, L_f, \ClockSet, I, E)$ be a timed automaton. We suppose that $\ClockSet$ is not empty and define (see \cref{figure:exists-opacity:PSPACE-hardness}) 
$\TA' = (\ActionSet, L \cup \set{\locinit', \loci{1}', \locfinal'}, \locinit', L_f, L_f \cup \set{\locfinal'}, \ClockSet, I', E')$ where $I'$ an invariant extending $I$ such that $I'(\locinit')=I'(\loci{1}') = I'(\locfinal')=\textit{true}$ and $E' = E \cup \set{(\locinit', \silentaction, (x=0), \emptyset, \locinit), 
(\locinit', \silentaction, (x=0), \emptyset, \loci{1}'), (\loci{1}', \silentaction, \textit{true}, \emptyset, \locfinal')} \cup \set{(\locfinal', a, \textit{true}, \emptyset, \locfinal') \mid a \in \ActionSet}$ for some $x \in \ClockSet$. 

