\section{Opacity of One-Action TAs}
\label{app:one-action}
%
%






\begin{proof}
We first prove the undecidability of the full opacity problem.
Let $\TA$ be a TA with a single action.
We want to build a TA such that if we can answer the full opacity problem of this TA, then we can decide the universality problem for~$\TA$.
We consider the following TA: we add an initial location exited by two 
$\silentaction$-transitions that must be taken urgently (\ie{} no time may elapse before taking them). The first $\silentaction$-transition leads to a secret location which leads (again via an urgent $\silentaction$-transition) to the initial location of the TA~$\TA$ and the other leads to a location where every finite timed words on $\ActionSet$ can be read before reaching a final location.
We denote this TA $\TB$ and illustrate its construction in \cref{figure:one-action:full-opacity}.
%
The language recognized by $\TA$ corresponds exactly to the traces of private runs of $\TB$, and the traces of public runs of $\TB$ are all the finite timed words on $\ActionSet$.
Therefore, $\TB$ is fully opaque if and only if $\PrivateTr{\TB} = \PublicTr{\TB}$ if and only if
$\Trace{\TA} = TW^*(\ActionSet)$ if and only if $\TA$ is universal.
%
%
Since universality for TAs with one action is undecidable~\cite{OW04}, we conclude that the full opacity problem for one-action~TAs is undecidable.

Finally, with \cref{th:weak-full-eq}, we deduce the undecidability of weak opacity for TAs with one action.
\qed
\end{proof}
