\begin{definition}[Square construction]
Let $\TA_{\textit{exp}} = (\ActionSet, L, \locinit, \emptyset, L_f, \ClockSet, I, E)$ be the timed automaton corresponding to the rational expression with square $\textit{exp}$. Then, the TA corresponding to $\textit{exp}^2$ is $\TA_{\textit{exp}^2} = (\ActionSet, L \cup\{\locinit', \locfinal'\}, \locinit', \emptyset, \set{\locfinal'}, \ClockSet \cup \set{v,y,z}, I', E')$ where 
%
%
$I'$ is the extension of $I$ such that $I'(\locinit')=I'(\locfinal')=\textit{true}$,
and
%
\begin{align*}
E' = & E \cup \set{(\locinit', \silentaction, \textit{true}, \emptyset, \locinit)} \cup  \\
& \bigcup\limits_{\loc \in L}^{} \set{(\loc, \silentaction, y=2, \set{y}, \loc), (\loc, \silentaction, v=2, \set{v}, \loc), 
(\loc, \silentaction, x=1 \wedge y=1, \set{v,y}, \loc)}\cup \\
&\bigcup\limits_{\locfinal \in L_f}^{} \{(\locfinal, \silentaction, x=0 \wedge y=1 \wedge v=1, \set{v}, \locinit'), \\
&\quad\quad\quad(\locfinal, \silentaction, z=0, \emptyset, \locfinal'), (\locfinal, \silentaction, x=0 \wedge y=1 \wedge v=0, \emptyset, \locfinal') \}.
\end{align*}
%
%
\end{definition}





Two rational languages with square $L_1$ and $L_2$, respectively represented by the expressions $\textit{exp}_1$ and $\textit{exp}_2$, are equal if and only if the automata $\TA_{\textit{exp}_1}$ and $\TA_{\textit{exp}_2}$ recognize the same timed language.
The obtained automata are timed automata with discrete time of polynomial size in the rational expressions. Thus from \cref{theorem:discrete-time:language-inclusion} follows the \EXPSPACE-hardness of language inclusion for discrete-time TA.\qed
\end{proof}









\opacdisc*

\begin{proof}
Let $\TA$ be a discrete-time automaton with private locations set $\PrivSet$. The construction in \cref{section:tools:Apriv-Apub} is still compatible with discrete time clocks so we can build two discrete-time TAs $\APriv$ and $\APub$ such that $\Trace{\APriv} = \PrivateTr{\TA}$ and $\Trace{\APub} = \PublicTr{\TA}$. Then testing the weak opacity property on $\TA$ is equivalent to testing the inclusion $\Trace{\APriv} \subseteq \Trace{\APub}$. 
Therefore the weak opacity problem in discrete-time TAs is in \EXPSPACE.

\EXPSPACE-hardness can easily be obtained by the following reduction:
Given two TA $\TA$ and $\TB$, one can build a TA which private runs are the runs of $\TA$ and which public ones are those of $\TB$. We do this by making the initial location of $\TA$ private and considering the natural construction of the union of $\TA$ and $\TB$. Hence comparing the languages of $\TA$ and $\TB$ amounts to testing weak opacity on the built automaton. 



As before, thanks to~\cref{th:weak-full-eq}, we can extend this result to the full opacity
problem.\qed
\end{proof}







