\section{\PSPACE-completeness of Weak / Full Opacity for oERAs}
\label{app:oera}
%
%



Let us first explain why the algorithm for weak opacity 
presented in the main document is in \PSPACE.
As a summary, this algorithm consists in, given an oERA $\TA$, building the corresponding 
$\AMemo$, determinizing it through the subset construction, taking its region automaton, and
then testing reachability of a location containing a final private location, but no final public location.

The determinization of the oERA causes the number of locations to become exponential in the size of the entry, and the construction of the region automaton gives an exponential number of clock regions, bounded by $\vert \ClockSet \vert \cdot 2^{\vert \ClockSet \vert} \cdot \prod\limits_{x \in \ClockSet}(2 M(x) + 2)$~\cite{AD94}.
The size of the region automaton is thus 
exponential in the number of locations and the number of clocks of $\TA$. 
On the region automaton, testing the reachability of a location can be done in \NLOGSPACE.  Hence the problem of weak opacity in oERA is in \PSPACE.

Let us now explain why these problems are \PSPACE-hard. We reduce from the reachability problem for TA, which is \PSPACE-complete.

Let $\TA$ be a timed automaton, with a set of final locations $L_f$. 
We consider $\TA'$ the TA obtained by setting in $\TA$ the set of private locations to $L_f$. This way, every run of $\TA'$ are private. Thus $\TA'$ is weakly opaque if and only if no final location of $\TA$ is reachable. Hence, the weak opacity problem in oERA is hence PSPACE-hard.





These results extend to full opacity thanks to \cref{th:weak-full-eq}.





%
%
\section{Opacity with $N$ Observations}
\label{app:lemtimedshape}
%
%



Given a $\TA = \TAprivextend$ and $N \in \setN$. 
We build the unfolding of the TA $\AMemo$ described in \cref{remark:APub-APriv:AMemo}.
Recall that $\AMemo$ recognizes the same language as $\TA$ but stores within the locations the information whether $\PrivSet$ was visited.
As such, $\AMemo$ has the same opacity properties as $\TA$, so we can consider 
$\Unfold{\AMemo}$ instead of $\Unfold{\TA}$ to study the opacity of~$\TA$.


Additionally, we enrich this TA with ticks. In \cref{section:opacity:discrete-time}, we added a single tick to the automaton which counted the time elapsed since the start of the run. Here, the TA includes as well, for each $0 < k \leq N$, a tick counting the time elapsed since the $k$'th observation. As multiple ticks may need to occur at the same time, we develop the alphabet of ticks to describe the set of tick clocks that need to be reset, \ie{} the tick $t_{\{k_1,\dots,k_m\}}$ is produced by the TA if for every $0 \leq i \leq m$, the $k_i$'th observation (or the start of the run if $k_i=0$) occurred an integer number of time units beforehand.
Note that the addition of these ticks immediately uses the assumption that only $N$ actions are observed.




\begin{definition}[Addition of ticks to the Unfolding construction]

	Let $\TA = \TAprivextend$ be a TA, $N \in \setN$ and let $\Unfold{\TA} = (\ActionSet, \LocSet', \loci{0}^0, \PrivSet', \FinalSet', \ClockSet, \invariant', \EdgeSet')$ the unfolding of $\TA$. We define the Tick construction $\Tick{\Unfold{\TA}} = (\ActionSet', \LocSet', \loci{0}^0, \PrivSet', \FinalSet', \ClockSet', \invariant', \EdgeSet'')$ where
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $\ActionSet' = \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0 \cup \ActionSet_t$ where $\ActionSet^0 = \set{a^0 \mid a \in \ActionSet}$ is a copy of the alphabet $\ActionSet$ 
that is used to represent within the action's name that it occurred at the same time as the previous action, and $\ActionSet_t = \set{t_K \mid K \subseteq \interval{0}{N}, K \neq \emptyset}$ is the set of ticks associated with each set of added clocks;
		\item $\ClockSet' = \ClockSet \cup \ClockSet_t$ where $\ClockSet_t = \set{x_i \mid i \in \interval{0}{N}}$ is the set of the $N+1$ tick clocks;
		\item $\EdgeSet'' = \bigcup\limits_{i=0}^{N-1} \EdgeSet^i \cup  \EdgeSet^{i \rightarrow i+1}$ is the set of transitions where, given $0 \leq i<N$
		\begin{itemize}
		\item $\EdgeSet^i = \lbrace (\loc^i, \silentaction, g \land \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{i}(x_k < 1), R, \loc'^i) \mid (\loc, \silentaction, g, R, \loc') \in \EdgeSet' \rbrace
		\cup
		\lbrace (\loc^i, t_K, \bigwedge\limits_{k \in K}(x_k = 1) \land \bigwedge\limits_{m \in \interval{0}{i} \setminus K}(0 < x_m < 1) , \lbrace x_k \mid k \in K \rbrace , \loc^i) \mid \loc^i \in \LocSet^i \land K \subseteq [\![0;i]\!] \land K \neq \emptyset \rbrace$;
		\item  $\EdgeSet^{i \rightarrow i+1} = \lbrace (\loc^i, a^0, g \land \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{i}(x_k < 1) \land \bigvee\limits_{m = 0}^{i} (x_m = 0), R \cup \lbrace x_{i+1} \rbrace, \loc'^{i+1}) \mid (\loc, a, g, R, \loc') \in \EdgeSet'\rbrace
		\cup
		\lbrace (\loc^i, a, g \land \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{i}(0 < x_k < 1), R \cup \lbrace x_{i+1} \rbrace, \loc'^{i+1}) \mid (\loc, a, g, R, \loc') \in \EdgeSet'\rbrace$.
		\end{itemize}
	\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

We obtain in this way the timed automaton $\TUM$.
Let $\RATUM$ be the region automaton of this automaton. 
Thanks to the added ticks, paths of $\RATUM$ sharing the same
trace correspond to runs of~$\TA$ for which the (at most) $N$ observations occurred
within the same time intervals (due to the tick representing the total time) and the fractional part of the timing of those observations have the same order.
This is the information we mainly need, and thus we wish to regroup every path of the region
automaton with the same trace.
As the region automaton is a finite automaton, we can realize usual operations on it,
that is, first remove $\silentaction$-transitions (by fusing them with the following 
non-$\silentaction$-transition) and then determinizing the automaton through the subset
construction. We denote by $\BA$ the resulting automaton.
We call \emph{beliefs} the states of~$\BA$, \ie{} they describe the set of regions the attacker believes the system may be in.

Let $B$ be a belief of $\BA$ and $B_{\mathit{priv}}$ (resp.\ $B_{\mathit{pub}}$) be the subset of $B$
containing the regions which associated location in $\AMemo$ is private (resp.\ public)
and final. We say that $B$ is weakly (resp.\ fully) \emph{discriminating} if
$B_{\mathit{priv}}\neq \emptyset$ and $B_{\mathit{pub}}=\emptyset$ (resp.\ if either
$B_{\mathit{priv}}\neq \emptyset$ and $B_{\mathit{pub}}=\emptyset$ or
$B_{\mathit{priv}}= \emptyset$ and $B_{\mathit{pub}}\neq\emptyset$).
The discriminating beliefs in $\BA$ allow to characterize the opacity problems.

%
\begin{proposition}[Relation between opacity and discriminating belief] \label{prop:discopac}
A TA $\TA$ is weakly (resp.\ fully) opaque \wrt{} $N$ observations if and only if $\BA$ does not contain any weakly
(resp.\ fully) discriminating belief.
\end{proposition}
%
\begin{proof}
We focus on weak opacity, the full opacity case can be treated similarly.
\begin{itemize}
\item Assume first that $\BA$ contains a weakly discriminating belief $B$. Let $r$ be
a region in $B_{\mathit{priv}}$ and $w$ be the trace of a path leading from the initial belief
of $\BA$ to $B$.
By construction of the region automaton, there exists a run $\run$ of $\TUM$ 
whose untimed trace (\ie{} the trace of $\run$ projected on the actions)
is $w$ and such that the run corresponding to $\run$ in the region
automaton ends in $r$. In particular, $\run$ is a private run.
Moreover, any run whose untimed trace is $w$ ends in a region of $B$. Thus, there is no
public run with trace $w$ and in particular 
$\Trace{\run}\in \PrivateTr{\TUM}$ and $\Trace{\run}\in \PublicTr{\TUM}$, hence
$\TUM$ is not weakly opaque and $\TA$ is not weakly opaque \wrt{} $N$ observations.


\item Assume now that $\TA$ is not weakly opaque \wrt{} $N$ observations.
Let $\run$ be a run of $\TUM$ such that 
$\Trace{\run}\in \PrivateTr{\TUM}$ and $\Trace{\run}\not \in \PublicTr{\TUM}$.
Let $[\run]$ be the run corresponding to $\run$ in the region automaton.\footnote{%
	The notation $[\cdot]$ represents that $[\run]$ implicitly defines an equivalence class of runs of $\TUM$. For a run $\rho'$ of $\TUM$, we thus write $\rho'\in[\rho]$ to say that the run associated with $\run'$ in the region automaton is $[\rho]$.
}
We denote by $T([\run])$ the set of traces of runs of $\TUM$ associated with $[\rho]$.



\begin{restatable}{lemma}{lemtimedshape}
\label{lem:correspticks}
Denoting $w=a_0,\dots, a_m$ the trace of $[\rho]$,
$T([\run])$ contains exactly the words 
$(a_0,\tau_0)\dots(a_m,\tau_m)$ satisfying the following constraints:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \label{lemma:timedshape:prop1}
$\forall i \in \interval{0}{m}, (a_i \in \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet_t \implies \tau_i - \tau_{i-1} > 0) \wedge (a_i \in \ActionSet^0 \implies \tau_i - \tau_{i-1} = 0)$
(where $\tau_{-1}=0$), meaning that two consecutive observable actions occur at the same time if and only if the second one is in $\ActionSet^0$.
\item \label{lemma:timedshape:prop2}
	$\forall i, j \in \interval{0}{m},\forall J \subseteq \interval{0}{N}, \forall I \subseteq J, \big(i<j \wedge a_i = t_I \wedge a_j = t_J \wedge \forall k \in \interval{i+1}{j-1}, \forall K \subseteq \interval{0}{N} (a_k = t_K \implies K \cap J = \emptyset) \big) \implies \tau_j - \tau_i = 1$,
meaning that two successive ticks of the same clocks are separated by exactly 1~time unit.
\item \label{lemma:timedshape:prop3}
	$\tau_m \geq 1 \implies \big(\exists i \in \interval{0}{m}, \exists I \subseteq \interval{0}{N} \forall
j < i, a_i=t_I \wedge 0\in I \wedge \tau_i=1 \wedge (a_j \not\in \ActionSet_t) \big)$,
meaning that the first occurrence of the tick of the clock $x_0$ is at time~1.
\item \label{lemma:timedshape:prop4}
$\forall i \in \interval{0}{m}, (a_i \in \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0 \wedge \tau_m - \tau_i \geq 1) \implies (\exists k \in \interval{0}{m}, 
\exists K \subseteq~\interval{0}{N}, 
a_k = t_K \wedge \vert \set{j\in \interval{0}{i} \mid a_j \in \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0} \vert \in K \wedge \tau_k - \tau_i = 1)$
meaning that each of the $N$ observations is followed by its corresponding tick
exactly one time unit after it.
\item \label{lemma:timedshape:prop5}
	$\forall i \in \interval{0}{m}, \forall I \subseteq \interval{0}{N}, (a_i = t_I \wedge \tau_m - \tau_i \geq 1) \implies \exists j \in \interval{i+1}{m}, \exists J \subseteq \interval{0}{N}, (I \subseteq J \wedge a_j = t_J)$
meaning that if a clock ticked and the run is still at least one time unit long, then there 
will be a new tick of this clock within the rest of the run.
\end{enumerate}
\end{restatable}
Due to its size, we postpone the proof of this lemma to the bottom of this section.


Note that this lemma implies that 
$T([\run])$ depends exclusively on the trace~$w$, not on the path within the region automaton. Hence, given $[\run']$ such that the trace of $[\run']$ is~$w$, we have $T([\run'])=T([\run])$.
In particular, let $B$ be the belief reached in $\BA$ with trace~$w$.
For any region $r\in B$ associated with a final location, there exists a run $\run'$ such that $\Trace{\run}=\Trace{\run'}$ and $[\run']$ ends in~$r$.
As $\Trace{\run}\not \in \PublicTr{\TUM}$ by assumption, we have that
$r$ is a region associated with a private location. Hence 
$B_{\mathit{priv}}\neq \emptyset$ and $B_{\mathit{pub}}=\emptyset$, thus $B$ is a weakly discriminating belief.\qed
\end{itemize}
\end{proof}




\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{th:Nfirstobs}]
From \cref{prop:discopac}, deciding weak and full opacity of $\TA$ amounts to checking the 
existence of a discriminating belief in~$\BA$.
This is simply achieved by a reachability
test in the finite automaton~$\BA$.

Considering the complexity, the unfolding of the TA, assuming $N$ is in binary, has 
exponentially many states. Adding the ticks means adding an exponential number of clocks as well. Hence the region automaton is doubly exponential and its determinisation is triply exponential. Reachability being in \NLOGSPACE implies the \twoEXPSPACE algorithm.
If $N$ is given in unary, the complexity falls to \EXPSPACE.
\qed
\end{proof}





































Let us finally establish \cref{lem:correspticks}.
For ease of readability, we separate the proofs of the two inclusions implying the lemma.

%
\subsubsection*{First direction of the proof}
%
%
We first show the easy direction of the proof: the timed words in $T([\run])$ satisfy the five properties.

\begin{proof}
 Let $u = (a_0, \tau_0) \dots (a_m, \tau_m)$ be in $T([\run])$. Since $u \in T([\run])$, there exists a run $\run'$ in $[\run]$ on $\TUM$ which produces the trace $u$: 
\[ \run' = (\loci{0}, \clockval_{0}) \longuefleche{(d_0, e_0)} \dots (\loci{j_i-1}, \clockval_{j_i-1}) \longuefleche{(d_{j_i-1}, e_{j_i-1})}(\loci{j_i}, \clockval_{j_i}) \dots (\loci{n}, \clockval_{n})\]
Recall the link between the trace of a run and its transitions: for every $i \in \interval{0}{m}$, the $j_{i}$ index corresponds to the $i$-th observable action, \ie{} $j_i$ satisfies $\tau_{i} = \sum\limits_{k=0}^{j_{i}}d_k$ and $e_{j_i}$ is labeled by $a_i$. We set $\tau_{-1} = 0$.


\begin{itemize}
	\item \emph{Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop1}.} Let $i \in \interval{0}{m}$. The set of clocks reset by $\silentaction$-transitions is included in~$\ClockSet$.
	By definition of the indices $j_i$, there are only $\silentaction$-transitions between the configurations $(\loci{j_{i-1}+1}, \clockval_{j_{i-1}+1})$ if $i>0$ or the initial configuration if $i=0$, and $(\loci{j_i}, \clockval_{j_i})$. Thus, no clock from $\ClockSet_t$ is reset among these transitions and we have $\clockval_{j_{i-1}+1}(x) +
\sum\limits_{k=j_{i-1}+1}^{j_i-1}d_k = \clockval_{j_{i}}(x)$ for each $x \in \ClockSet_t$. Assume $a_i \in \ActionSet^0$.
As $a_i \in \ActionSet^0$, the guard of $e_{j_i}$ is of the form $g \land \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h}(x_k < 1) \land \bigvee\limits_{k = 0}^{h} (x_k = 0)$ with some guard $g$ and the copy number $h \leq i$ of $e_{j_i} \in E^{h \rightarrow h+1}$ (in other words, the integer $h$ such that the transition of the original automaton starts in the copy $L^h$). In particular, there exists a clock $x \in \ClockSet_t$ such that $\clockval_{j_i}(x) + d_{j_i} =0$.
Therefore we obtain $\clockval_{j_{i-1}+1}(x) + 
\sum\limits_{k=j_{i-1}+1}^{j_i}d_k = 0$, 
which implies $\tau_i - \tau_{i-1} = 0$.




Let us now assume that $a_i \in \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet_t$, and show that $\tau_i - \tau_{i-1} > 0$. Depending on whether $a_i$ is in $\ActionSet$ or in $\ActionSet_t$, the guard of the transition $e_{j_i}$ can be of two forms :   $g \land \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h}(0 < x_k < 1)$ with $g$ some guard and $h$ some copy number, if $a_i \in \ActionSet$; or $\bigwedge\limits_{k \in K}(x_k = 1) \land \bigwedge\limits_{m \in \interval{0}{h} \setminus K}(0 < x_m < 1)$ with $K \subseteq \interval{0}{h}$ non-empty and $h$ a copy number, if $a_i \in \ActionSet_t$. In both cases, we must have $\clockval_{j_i}(x_k) + d_{j_i} > 0$ for each $x_k \in \ClockSet_t$ such that $k \leq h$. However the last observable transition $e_{j_{i-1}}$ resets one of those clocks $x$, which gives $\clockval_{j_{i-1}+1}(x) = 0$. Hence 
$0<\clockval_{j_i}(x) + d_{j_i} = \clockval_{j_{i-1}+1}(x)+ \sum\limits_{k=j_{i-1}}^{j_i}d_k = \sum\limits_{k=j_{i-1}}^{j_i}d_k$ .


\item \emph{Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop2}}. Let $i, i' \in \interval{0}{m}$, with $i<i'$. Assume there are $I' \subseteq \interval{0}{N}$ and $I \subseteq I'$ such that $a_i = t_I$ and $a_{i'} = t_{I'}$. Suppose that for every $k \in \interval{i+1}{i'-1}$ and for each $K \subseteq \interval{0}{N}$, $a_k = t_K \implies K\cap I'=\emptyset$. Let us show that $\tau_{i'}-\tau_i = 1$.

Let $x \in \set{x_k \mid k \in I} \subseteq \ClockSet_t$. After the reset applied by $e_{j_i}$, we have: $\clockval_{j_i+1} = \reset{\clockval_{j_i} + d_{j_i}}{\set{x_k \mid k \in I}} \models (x=0)$. In order to take the transition $e_{j_{i'}}$, the clock valuation  $\clockval_{j_{i'}} +d_{j_{i'}}$ needs to satisfy the guard $(x=1)$. 
It remains to show that $x$ is not reset between $e_{j_i}$ and $e_{j_{i'}}$. Let $e$ be a transition between $e_{j_i}$ and~$e_{j_{i'}}$.
If $e$ is an $\silentaction$-transition, it does not reset any clock in $\ClockSet_t$. If $e$ is labeled by a letter in $\ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0$, it is in some $\EdgeSet^{h'\rightarrow h'+1}$ with $h' \geq h$ and $h$ the copy number of the configuration preceding $e_{j_i}$. The only clock $e$ resets that is not in $\ClockSet$ is $x_{h'+1}$, and cannot be $x$ since $I \subseteq \interval{0}{h}$.
Finally, if $e = (\loc, t_K, g, R, \loc')$ is labeled by a tick $t_K$ for some $K \subseteq \interval{0}{N}$, the hypothesis $K \cap I' = \emptyset$ in the property ensures that $x$ is not part of the set of clocks reset by $e$. Hence, we obtain $\clockval_{j_{i'}}(x) + d_{j_{i'}} = \reset{\clockval_{j_i} + d_{j_i}}{\set{x_k \mid k \in I}} + \sum\limits_{k=j_i +1}^{j_{i'}}d_k  = \tau_{i'} - \tau_i = 1$, which concludes the proof of the second point.

\item \emph{Properties~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop3} and~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop4}}.
Both Properties~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop3} and~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop4} are similar and require the first tick of a clock in $\ClockSet_t$ to occur one time unit after reaching the corresponding copy of the automaton. Indeed Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop3} is the particular case of $h=0$ in the following proof, and Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop4} is the case $h>0$.
Let $h \in \interval{0}{N}$. We focus on the clock $x_h \in \ClockSet_t$. Let $i$ be the index of the $h$-th observation in the trace, that is to say the $h$-th letter of the trace that is in $\ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0$. If $h=0$, we set $i=-1$. In any cases $\tau_i$ is the time of arrival in the copy $h$ via the transition $e_{j_i} \in E^{h-1\rightarrow h}$, and at this precise moment $x_h = 0$.
Every guard of the transitions of $E^{h'}$ and $E^{h'\rightarrow h'+1}$ with $h' \geq h$ requires that $x_h <1$, except for the transitions labeled by $t_K$ with $h \in K$, which reset $x_h$ and require $x_h=1$. Thus, $x_h$ must have been reset if the run lasts more than one time unit after the $h$-th observation, \ie{} if $\tau_m - \tau_i \geq 1$. The only transitions from the copy $h$ that can reset $x_h$ are those labeled by $t_K$ with $h \in K$; their guards require $x_h = 1$ so the first reset of $x_h$ after reaching the copy $h$ needs to occur at time $\tau_i +1$.

\item \emph{Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop5}}.
	We use the same argument as Properties~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop3} and~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop4} to prove the fifth property.
Let $i \in \interval{0}{m}$, and suppose $a_i = t_I$ for some non-empty $I \subseteq \interval{0}{N}$ and $\tau_m - \tau_i \geq 1$. The transitions following $e_{j_i}$ require $\bigwedge\limits_{k \in I}(x_k<1)$, unless they reset all the clocks in $R_I = \set{x_k \mid k \in I}$. As $\tau_m - \tau_i \geq 1$, an observable action occurs at least one time unit after $e_{j_i}$ and the clocks of $R_I$ thus needed to be reset. Hence there is a tick transition labeled by $t_{J}$ with $I \subseteq J$ happening after $e_{j_i}$.
\end{itemize}
\qed
\end{proof}








%
\subsubsection*{Second direction of the proof}
%
%
Now we tackle the second direction of the proof: we show that if $a_0 \dots a_m$ is the trace of a run $[\run]$, then all the timed words $(a_0, \tau_0) \dots (a_m, \tau_m)$ that satisfy the five properties of the lemma are in $T([\run])$, \ie{} there are runs in $[\run]$ which produce these timed traces.


\begin{proof}
Let $w = a_0 \dots a_m$ be a trace in $\RATUM$, and let $\tau = (\tau_0, \dots, \tau_m)$ be a timestamps sequence such that the timed word $(a_0, \tau_0) \dots (a_m, \tau_m)$ verifies the five properties of the lemma. We define the sequence $(f_i)_{0\leq i \leq N}$ of the timestamps' fractional parts associated with each tick clock.
 %
For each $i \in \interval{0}{N}$ we set $J_i = \set{j \in \interval{0}{m} \bigmid \exists I \subseteq \interval{0}{N}, i \in I \wedge a_j = t_I}$. Let $\textit{first}(i) = \text{min} \set{j \in \interval{0}{m} \bigmid \vert \set{k \in \interval{0}{j} \bigmid a_k \in \ActionSet \cup \ActionSet^0} \vert = i}$ (be the subscript of the $i$-th observation) if $i>0$ and $\textit{first}(0) = -1$. Thus $\textit{first}(i)$ is the subscript of the first observation in $w$ that resets the clock $x_i \in \ClockSet_t$. We set $f_i = \fract{\tau_{\textit{first}(i)}}$. 
Then, from Properties~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop2} and~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop4} we have that for each $i$ in $\interval{0}{N}$ and for each $j \in J_i$, $\fract{\tau_j} = f_i$.
From Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop3}, we get $f_0 = 0$.


If $i,i' \in \interval{0}{N}$ with $i<i'$, then Property~\ref{lemma:timedshape:prop1} ensures that $f_i = f_{i'}$ if and only if there exist $j \in J_i \cup \set{\textit{first}(i)}$ such that $j+1 = \textit{first}(i')$ and $a_{\textit{first}(i')} \in \ActionSet^0$.
Note that the sequence $(f_i)_{0 \leq i \leq N}$ depends only on the timed trace and not on the path in the region automaton.

Let $[\run] : r_0, b_0, r_1, \dots b_{p-1} r_p$ be a run in $\TUM$ of trace $w$. For $n \in \interval{0}{p}$ we define (deduced from $\tau$) the constraint $C_{\tau, r_n}$ on the tick clocks, in order to produce a run of $[\run]$ that corresponds to $\tau$. We denote by $\loc_n^h$ the location of $r_n$ with $h$ the number of the copy of $L$ it belongs to, and by $[\clockval_n]$ the clock region of $r_n$.

We distinguish two cases depending on whether $r_n$ is a region where at least one tick clock has integer value (noting that, by property of the region automaton, if one valuation of $[\clockval_n]$ give an integer value to a clock, then all valuations of $[\clockval_n]$ do).
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{$\exists x_i\in\ClockSet_t, \clockval_n(x_i)\in \setN$%
:} In this case and if $i \leq h$ we set \textit{(type 1 constraint)} 
\begin{align*}
C_{\tau, r_n} &= \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h} ((f_k \leq f_i \implies \fract{x_k} = f_i - f_k)\\ & \wedge (f_i < f_k \implies \fract{x_k} = f_i - f_k + 1))\\ & \wedge \bigwedge\limits_{k = h+1}^{N} (\fract{x_k} = x_0)
\end{align*}
%

%
%
%

\item \textbf{$\forall x_i\in\ClockSet_t, \clockval_n(x_i)\notin \setN$:}

Let $i$ be the index of one of the last tick clocks that were reset in $[\run]$ before $r_n$. 
%
If there is no tick clock reset before $r_n$, we set $i=0$.
Similarly, we consider $j \in \interval{0}{h+1}$ one of the clock indices of the next tick clock reset after $r_n$. If there is no tick clock reset after $r_n$, we take the next tick clock reset that is supposed to occur, \ie{} $j = \text{min}~\set{j' \in \interval{1}{h} \bigmid f_i < f_{j'}}$ or $j=0$ if this set is empty.

If $j \in J_0$, we set \textit{(type 2 constraint)}
\[ C_{\tau, r_n} = \exists \delta \in (0;1) \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h} (f_i - f_k < x_k < 1-f_k \wedge 1-f_k-x_k=\delta) \wedge \bigwedge\limits_{k = h+1}^{N} (\fract{x_k} = x_0)\]

Otherwise, and if we have $0 < j < h+1$ and $j \notin J_0$, we set
\textit{(type 3 constraint)} 
\begin{align*}
C_{\tau, r_n} &= \exists \delta \in (0;1) \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h} ((f_k < f_j \implies (f_i - f_k < x_k < f_j-f_k \wedge f_j - f_k - x_k = \delta)) \\ &\wedge (f_j \leq f_k \implies (f_i - f_k + 1 < x_k < f_j - f_k + 1 \wedge f_j-f_k+1-x_k = \delta))) \\& 
\wedge \bigwedge\limits_{k = h+1}^{N} (\fract{x_k} = x_0)
\end{align*} 

If $j = h+1$ we operate a slight change on this formula to obtain the following one: \textit{(type 4 constraint)}
\begin{align*}
C_{\tau, r_n} &= \exists \delta \in (0;1) \bigwedge\limits_{k = 0}^{h} ((f_k < f_{h+1} \implies (f_i - f_k < x_k \leq f_{h+1}-f_k \wedge f_{h+1} - f_k - x_k = \delta)) \\ &\wedge (f_{h+1} < f_k \implies (1 - f_k < x_k \leq f_{h+1} - f_k + 1 \wedge f_{h+1}-f_k+1-x_k = \delta))) \\& 
\wedge \bigwedge\limits_{k = h+1}^{N} (\fract{x_k} = x_0)
\end{align*}
%

\end{itemize}

Now we combine this information to the constraints of the clock regions of a run in the region automaton to build a set of runs in $\TUM$.

For $n \in \interval{0}{p}$ we define the set of valuations \[M_n := \set{\clockval \in [\clockval_n] \mid \clockval \models C_{\tau, r_n}}.\] We denote by $[\run]_\tau$ the subset of $[\run]$ defined by $r'_0, b_0, r'_1, \dots b_{p-1} r'_p$ where for each $n \in \interval{0}{p}, r'_n = (\loc_n^h, M_n)$. 
The idea is that the successive clock valuations of runs of $[\run]_\tau$ are in these sets $M_n$ of valuations which correspond to region changes: 
a tick clock reaches or exits an integer value each time the next clock valuation $\clockval'_{n+1}$ does no more satisfy $C_{\tau, r_n}$. %

We prove by induction that $[\run]_{\tau}$ is not empty. The set $M_0$ contains at least $\clockval_0$ since it verifies $C_{\tau, r_0} = (\fract{x_0} = 0) \wedge \bigwedge\limits_{k=1}^N \fract{x_k} = 0$. 
Assume now there is a path $(\loci{0}^0, \clockval'_0), b_0, \dots, (\loci{n}^h, \clockval'_n)$ with $\clockval'_j \in M_j$ for each $j \in \interval{0}{n}$. 
We show there exists $\clockval'_{n+1} \in M_{n+1}$ such that $(\loci{0}^0, \clockval'_0), b_0, \dots, (\loci{n+1}^h, \clockval'_{n+1})$ is a path in $r'_0, b_0, r'_1 \dots r'_{n+1}$. 
It is well known \cite{Bouyer21} that in the region automaton, $((\loc, [\clockval]), a, (\loc', [\clockval'])) \in \EdgeSet_R$ if and only if \underline{for all} $\clockval \in [\clockval]$ there exists $\clockval' \in [\clockval']$ such that $(\loc, \clockval) \transitionWith{e} (\loc', \clockval') \in E$, with $e$ being the transition associated with $((\loc, [\clockval]), a, (\loc', [\clockval']))$ in the timed automaton. Since $\clockval'_n \in [\clockval_n]$, by the above property we can find $\clockval'_{n+1} \in [\clockval_{n+1}]$ following the transition $(r_n, b_n, r_{n+1})$. We show that there is such a $\clockval'_{n+1}$ that is also in $M_{n+1}$, \ie{} such that $\clockval'_{n+1} \models C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$.


\begin{itemize}
\item %
Suppose first $(r_n, b_n, r_{n+1})$ is a discrete transition, with reset set $R$. Only one clock valuation $\clockval'_{n+1}$ can succede to $\clockval'_n$ in this case. We show it is in $M_{n+1}$. If $b_n \in \ActionSet$ (resp. $\ActionSet^0$), then $\clockval'_n$ verifies a type 1 (resp. 4) constraint. We move to copy $h+1$ with a reset of some tick clocks (including $x_{h+1}$) so the next constraint to be verified is of type 1. The clock valuation $\clockval'_{n+1}$ is entirely determined by $\clockval'_n$ and the clock reset set. %

Since we move to copy $h+1$, the clock $x_{h+1}$ is now part of the tick clocks indexed from 0 to the current copy number, and $\clockval'_{n+1}(x_{h+1}) = 0$, which is required by the constraint $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$. Moreover the constraints on the other clocks did not change. Thus the new type 1 constraint $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$ is verified by $\clockval'_{n+1}$.
%
Now, if $b_n \in \ActionSet_t$, only clocks reaching 1 are reset so their fractional part is not affected and the clock valuation $\clockval'_{n+1}$ still satisfies $C_{\tau, r_n}$. The constraint $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$ depends on the time elapsed since the last tick and whether the transition was an observation, so in this case $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}} = C_{\tau, r_{n}}$.
Finally, if $b_n$ is an $\silentaction$-transition, it only resets clocks from $\ClockSet$ and we have again $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}} = C_{\tau, r_{n}}$. Since this constraint restricts only clocks from $\ClockSet_t$ and since their valuation does not change, $\clockval'_{n+1}$ still verifies $C_{\tau, r_n}$ and is in $M_{n+1}$.
\item %
We now assume that $(r_n, b_n, r_{n+1})$ is a delay transition: 
there is $d_n \in (0;1)$ such that $\clockval'_{n+1} = \clockval'_n + d_n$. This delay must verify some conditions because $\clockval'_n$ is fixed and a transition can change region only once. In all this paragraph, we take $i$ (resp. $j$) some index of the last (resp. next) reset tick clocks. Suppose that $C_{\tau, r_{n}}$ is a constraint of type 2, 3 or 4. 
Assume in the first case that $x_j \in \ClockSet_t$ is such that $\clockval_{n+1}(x_j) \in \setN$ (so the next constraint $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$ is of type 1). Thus this clock has reached 1 ($\clockval'_{n+1}(x_j) = 1$) and $C_{\tau, r_n}$ is a type 2 or 3 constraint. This setting entirely determines the unique reachable clock valuation $\clockval'_{n+1}$. From the fact that $\clockval'_n$ models $C_{\tau, r_n}$, we have some $\delta \in (0;1)$ such that $1 + f_j - f_j - \clockval_n'(x_j) = \delta$. Consequently $d_n = \delta$. We easily verify that the obtained $\clockval_{n+1}$ satisfies $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$. Now if the next clock to be reset is $x_{h+1}$ and there is no other delay transition before the next observation, then $C_{\tau, r_n}$ is a type 4 constraint and we need to have $\clockval'_{n+1}(x_{h+1}) = f_{h+1}$, which means that $d_n = f_{h+1} - \clockval'_n(x_0)$. The next constraint $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$ is still the same as $C_{\tau, r_n}$. We have some $\delta$ for $C_{\tau, r_n}$ and obtain the new $\delta' = \delta - d_n = 0$ to satisfy $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$.

Suppose now that there will be at least one other delay transition before the next tick clock reset. Assume $C_{\tau, r_n}$ is of type 2, 3 or 4. Then, if this transition changes region, it only concerns clocks from $\ClockSet$ and we have $C_{\tau, r_n} = C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$.
The preceding constraint gives $\delta = 1 - \clockval'_n(x_j)$ if $C_{\tau, r_n}$ is of type 2 or 3 and $\delta = f_j - \clockval'_n(x_j)$ if it is of type 4. The delay $d_n$ cannot reach $\delta$ (otherwise $\clockval'_{n+1}(x_j) \in \setN$ or the transition crosses more than one clock region at once, two excluded cases). We can construct the new $\delta' = \delta - d_n$ involved in $\clockval'_{n+1} \models C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$ and verify that $\clockval'_{n+1}$ is thus still in $M_{n+1}$. 
Assume now that $C_{\tau,r_n}$ is of type 1. Then the next region must give a constraint of type 2, 3 or 4. This case is similar to the last tackled one, and for the same reasons the delay $d_n$ cannot reach $1 - f_i + f_*$ where $f_* \in \set{- f_j,1-f_j}$. In both cases we obtain from the $\delta$ of $C_{\tau, r_n}$ the new one $\delta' = \delta - d_n$ for $C_{\tau, r_{n+1}}$, which is verified.
\end{itemize}
Hence and by induction, $[\run]_{\tau}$ is not empty.

It remains to show that the timed trace these runs produce is $(a_0, \tau_0) \dots (a_m, \tau_m)$ (recall that is is the trace of $[\run]$ timed by a timestamp $\tau$ which verifies the five properties of the lemma). Let $\run' \in [\run]_{\tau}$. Let $(a_i, \tau_i)$ be a letter of the timed trace. Suppose the action $a_i$ occurs in $\run'$ at time $\tau'_i$ following the clock region $[\clockval_{j_i}]$. Then the number of ticks of $x_0$ before $a_i$ is given by the trace and is exactly $\lfloor \tau_i \rfloor = \lfloor \tau'_i \rfloor$.
%
	We know that $\fract{\tau'_i} = \fract{\clockval_{j_i}(x_0)}$. Moreover, $a_i$ occurs at the same time as the reset of a clock $x_{k}$. If $a_i$ is a tick, the constraint of $M_{j_i}$ forces $\clockval_{j_i}(x_{0}) = f_{k}$ and we have $i \in J_k$ so $\fract{\tau'_i} = f_{k} = \fract{\tau_{i}}$. Otherwise $a_i$ is an observation, so there exists $h \in \interval{0}{N-1}$ such that $i = \textit{first}(h+1)$. The clock $x_{h+1}$ has never been reset yet so $\tau'_i = \clockval_{j_i}(x_{h+1})$. The constraint of $M_{j_i}$ gives
%
	$\fract{\clockval_{j_i}(x_{h+1})} = f_{h+1}$ and $\fract{\clockval_{j_i}(x_{0})} = f_{h+1}$. Since $i = \textit{first}(h+1)$, we obtain $\fract{\tau_{i}} = f_{h+1} = \clockval_{j_i}(x_{0}) = \fract{\tau'_{i}}$.\qed
\end{proof}


