    \subsection{Learning by Teaching Vs Self-practice}

    We compared the learning outcomes of learning-by-teaching a social robot with the self-practice condition, while also examining whether the perception of the task differed between the two approaches.

    Figure~\ref{fig:H2_gain} shows that, on average, participants in the LbT condition achieved higher post- and retention gains compared to those in the Self-Practice condition. This difference was significant in the Body Parts Game, where participants in the LbT condition ($M=0.90, SE =3.89$) demonstrated a higher retention gain than those in the Self-Practice condition ($M=-1.70, SE = 4.78$), $t(50) = 2.16, p<0.05, r=0.29$. 

