\subsection{Analytic Conditional Value Function}
The remaining ingredients are the analytic forms of the conditional value function and the combination rules. 
To derive them, we start by reformulating \eqref{eq:con_value_fun} into a dense form by substituting the states with the inputs, $\boldsymbol{u}_k = (u_n)_{n \in [k, i-1]}$. 
The optimization problem is then
\begin{equation} \label{eq:con_val_fun_dense}
\begin{aligned}
    V_{k\to i}(x_k, x_i) \\ = \min_{\boldsymbol{u}_k}\; & 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        g_{q,k} \\  g_{r,k} 
    \end{bmatrix} ^\top 
    \begin{bmatrix} x_k \\ \boldsymbol{u}_k \end{bmatrix} + \frac{1}{2}
    \begin{bmatrix} x_k \\ \boldsymbol{u}_k \end{bmatrix} ^\top 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        H_{Q,k} &  H_{M,k}^\top\\ H_{M,k} & H_{R,k}
    \end{bmatrix} 
    \begin{bmatrix} x_k \\ \boldsymbol{u}_k \end{bmatrix}
    \\ 
    \text{s.t.}\;  &x_i = \bar{A}_k x_k + \bar{B}_k\boldsymbol{u}_k + \bar{c}_k,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
with one equality constraint ensuring the system reaches $x_i$ at time step $i$.
Since the exact expressions for the gradient $\left(g_{q,k}, g_{r,k}\right)$, Hessian $\left( H_{Q,k}, H_{R,k}, H_{M,k} \right)$, and dynamics matrices $\left(  \bar{A}_k, \bar{B}_k \right)$ are not essential for our purpose, we omit them here for brevity and refer the reader to \cite{FrisonCondensing2016} for further details.
To solve \eqref{eq:con_val_fun_dense}, we apply the duality theorem and derive the solution to the inner minimization problem by setting the gradient to zero. This leads to
\begin{equation} 
    V_{k\to i}(x_{k},x_{i})=\max_{\lambda }\;g_{k\to i}(\lambda;x_{k},x_{i}), \label{eq:dual_problem}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x}$ represents the Lagrange multiplier associated with the equality constraint in \eqref{eq:con_val_fun_dense}. 
The solution to the minimization problem, denoted by $g_{k\to i}$, is parameterized by $x_k$ and $x_i$. Its exact expression is given by:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sol_min}
\begin{aligned} 
    g_{k\to i}(\lambda;x_{k},x_{i})= z & +\frac{1}{2}x_{k}^{\top}P_{k,i}x_{k} + p_{k,i}^{\top} x_{k} \\ &-\frac{1}{2}\lambda ^{\top }C_{k,i}\lambda {+}\lambda ^{\top }\left(x_{i}{-}A_{k,i}x_{k}{-}c_{k,i}\right),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{T}_{k,i} \triangleq (P_{k,i}, p_{k,i}, C_{k,i}, A_{k,i}, c_{k,i})$ can be expressed in terms of the gradient,
Hessian and dynamics matrices in \eqref{eq:con_val_fun_dense} or using the recursion introduced in \cite{wright_partitioned_1991}. 
More technical details are provided in  Appendix \ref{subsec:dual_value_function}. 
If $i > k+1$, we can perform the decomposition and express $\mathcal{T}_{k,i}$ in terms of $\mathcal{T}_{k,j}$ and $\mathcal{T}_{j,i}$.
To achieve this, we plug \eqref{eq:sol_min} into \eqref{eq:V_ki_min}, yielding
\begin{align}
    &V_{k\to i}(x_k, x_i) = \nonumber \\ & = \min_{x_j}\, \left\{ \max_{\lambda_1 }\,g_{k\to j}(\lambda_1;x_{k}, x_{j}) + \max_{\lambda_2 }\,g_{j\to i}(\lambda_2;x_{j},x_{i}) \right\} \nonumber \\
    &= \max_{\lambda_1, \lambda_2} \min_{x_j} g_{k\to j}(\lambda_1;x_{k}, x_{j}) + g_{j\to i}(\lambda_2;x_{j},x_{i}) \nonumber \\
    &= \max_{\lambda_2} \underbrace{\left\{ \max_{\lambda_1} \min_{x_j} g_{k\to j}(\lambda_1;x_{k}, x_{j}) + g_{j\to i}(\lambda_2;x_{j},x_{i}) \right\}}_{g_{k\to i}(\lambda_2;x_{k},x_{i})}  \label{eq:V_ki_analytic}
\end{align}
We then derive the closed-form solution to the min-max problem within the curly brackets in \eqref{eq:V_ki_analytic} and align the resulting matrices with those in \eqref{eq:sol_min}.
We present the full derivation in Appendix \ref{subsec:comb_rule} and summarize the resulting relationships below:
% \begin{equation} 
% \begin{split} \label{eq:comb_rule}
% A_{k,i} &= A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} A_{k,j}, \\ c_{k,i} &= A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} (c_{k,j} + C_{k,j} p_{j,i}) + c_{j,i}, \\ 
% C_{k,i} &= A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} C_{k,j} A_{j,i}^\top + C_{j,i}, \\ 
% p_{k,i} &= A_{k,j}^\top (I + P_{j,i} C_{k,j})^{-1} (p_{j,i} - P_{j,i} b_{k,j}) + p_{k,j}, \\ 
% P_{k,i} &= A_{k,j}^\top (I + P_{j,i} C_{k,j})^{-1} P_{j,i} A_{k,j} + P_{k,j}.
% \end{split}  
% \end{equation}
\begin{equation} 
\begin{split} \label{eq:comb_rule}
A_{k,i} &= -A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} A_{k,j}, \\ 
c_{k,i} &= A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} (-c_{k,j} + C_{k,j} p_{j,i}) + c_{j,i}, \\ 
C_{k,i} &= A_{j,i} (I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1} C_{k,j} A_{j,i}^\top + C_{j,i}, \\ 
p_{k,i} &= A_{k,j}^\top (I + P_{j,i} C_{k,j})^{-1} (p_{j,i} + P_{j,i} b_{k,j}) + p_{k,j}, \\ 
P_{k,i} &= A_{k,j}^\top (I + P_{j,i} C_{k,j})^{-1} P_{j,i} A_{k,j} + P_{k,j}.
\end{split}  
\end{equation}
We note that $(I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1}$ is the transpose of $(I + P_{j,i} C_{k,j})^{-1}$, meaning that only a single LU factorization is required to compute the matrices in \eqref{eq:comb_rule}. 
Moreover, $(I + C_{k,j} P_{j,i})^{-1}$ is nonsingular, as  both $C_{k,j}$ and $P_{j,i}$ are positive definite.
Next, we discuss the one-step conditional value function $V_{k\to k+1}$, which represents the smallest subproblem and serves as an element in the input array for the parallel scan.
By considering the dual problem in \eqref{eq:dual_problem} again, we can express the matrices in $V_{k\to k+1}$ as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:back_init}
\begin{aligned} 
    P_{k, k+1} &= Q_k - M_kR_k^{-1} M_k^{\top}, \\ p_{k, k+1} &= q_k -  M_k R_k^{-1} r_k, \\
    C_{k, k+1} &= B_k R_k^{-1} B_{k}, \\
    A_{k, k+1} &= A_k - B_k R_k^{-1}M_k^\top, \\
    c_{k, k+1} &= c_k - B_kR_k^{-1}r_k.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since the cost weight matrix $R_k$ is typically diagonal and positive definite, its inverse can be easily computed.

\section{Parallel Dense LQR} \label{sec:parallel_dense_lqr}
The parallel method described in the previous section is well-suited for long-horizon problems, such as $P_1$ and $P_1^i$, but may incur significant parallelization overhead in short-horizon cases, such as $P_2$.
Therefore, we can alternatively solve the dense form of \eqref{eq:LQR} obtained by eliminating the state variables. 
Since we aim to solve multiple LQR problems, the condensing procedure must be performed online, unlike in \cite{Bishop2024ReLUQP, ColeGPULQR2023}. 
The major computational overhead consists of the condensing procedure and the factorization of the linear system. 
As noted in \cite{ColeGPULQR2023}, \textsc{cuSolver} \cite{cuda} efficiently performs dense matrix factorization. 
Therefore, the main challenge lies in the condensing process. 
To optimize this process for GPU execution, we parallelize it over time.

We now delve into technical details.
The state sequence $\boldsymbol{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)n_x}$ can be expressed in terms of the input sequence $\boldsymbol{u} \in \mathbb{R}^{Nn_u}$ as follows:  
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{x} = \Phi_{\text{p}}x_0 + S_{\text{p}}\boldsymbol{u} + F_{\text{p}}\boldsymbol{c} \label{eq:pred_states}
\end{align} 
where the prediction matrices $\Phi_{\text{p}} \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)n_x \times n_x}$, $ S_{\text{p}} \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)n_x \times Nn_x}$ and $S_{\text{p}} \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)n_x \times Nn_u}$ are derived as
\begin{align*}
    \Phi_{\text{p}} &:= \begin{bmatrix}I \\ A_0 \\ A_1A_0 \\ \vdots \\
    \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{0}A_k \end{bmatrix},
    F_{\text{p}} := \begin{bmatrix}
    0 & 0   & \cdots & 0 \\
    I & 0  & \cdots & 0 \\
    A_1 & I  & \cdots & 0 \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & 0 \\
    \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{1}A_k & \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{2}A_k & \cdots & I
    \end{bmatrix}, \\
    S_{\text{p}} &:= \begin{bmatrix}
    0 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\
    B_0 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\
    A_1B_0 & B_1 &  \cdots & 0 \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & 0 \\
     \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{1}A_k B_0 & \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{2}A_k B_1 & \cdots & B_{N-1} 
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Since generating these prediction matrices resembles executing a linear rollout, we can apply the parallel scan again.
To generate $\Phi_{\text{p}}$, we consider the input array $\left[ I, A_0, A_1, \cdots,  A_{N-1} \right]$, 
with the combination rule defined as
$A_i \oplus A_j = A_j A_i$.
For $F_{\text{p}}$, we first introduce the following auxiliary matrices:
\begin{align*}
    F_{\text{p}, 0} := \begin{bmatrix}
    I & I   & \cdots & I \\
    I & I  & \cdots & I \\
    A_1 & I  & \cdots & I \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & I \\
    A_1 & A_2 & \cdots & I
    \end{bmatrix},
    F_{\text{p}, \text{c}} := \begin{bmatrix}
    -I & -I   & \cdots & -I \\
    0 & -I  & \cdots & -I \\
    0 & 0  & \cdots & -I \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & -I \\
    0 & 0 & \cdots & 0
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}    
We then apply the reverse parallel scan to each block-wise row of $F_{\text{p},0}$, resulting in
\begin{align*}
    F_{\text{p}, 1} := \begin{bmatrix}
    I & I   & \cdots & I \\
    I & I  & \cdots & I \\
    A_1 & I  & \cdots & I \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & I \\
    \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{1}A_k & \prod \limits_{k=N-1}^{2}A_k & \cdots & I
    \end{bmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Note that the computation of each row is independent and can be performed in parallel.
Finally, we obtain $F_{\text{p}}$ by adding $F_{\text{p}, 1}$ and $F_{\text{p}, \text{c}}$. 
Subsequently, $S_{\text{p}}$ is generated from $F_{\text{p}}$ by multiplying the $i\text{-th}$ block-wise column of $F_{\text{p}}$ with $B_i$. Next, we let $\bar{Q}$, $\bar{R}$ and $\bar{M}$ denote the block-diagonal cost matrices in \eqref{eq:LQR}. 
By substituting \eqref{eq:pred_states} into \eqref{eq:LQR}, we obtain the dense LQR formulation:
\begin{align}
    \min_{\boldsymbol{u}}\; \frac{1}{2} \boldsymbol{u}^\top H \boldsymbol{u} + h^\top \boldsymbol{u}, \label{eq:condensed}
\end{align}
where $H \in \mathbb{R}^{Nn_u \times Nn_u} := S_{\text{p}}^\top \bar{Q} S_{\text{p}} + \bar{R} + 2 \bar{M} S_{\text{p}}$ and $h \in \mathbb{R}^{Nn_u} := S_{\text{p}}^\top \bar{q} + (\bar{M} + S_{\text{p}}^\top \bar{Q}) F_{\text{p}} \bar{c} + \bar{r} + \left( \bar{M} \Phi_{\text{p}} + S_{\text{p}}^\top \bar{Q} \Phi_{\text{p}} \right) x_0$.
Moreover, these matrix multiplications can be optimized by exploiting the sparse structure of the cost matrices. 

\section{Parallel Branch MPC} \label{sec:parallel_bmpc}
In this section, we combine the methods presented in Sections \ref{sec:parallel_lqr} and \ref{sec:parallel_dense_lqr} to develop an efficient BMPC solver for \eqref{eq:bmpc}.
We adopt an iLQR-based method, where the original problem in \eqref{eq:bmpc} is addressed by iteratively solving a sequence of LQR-Tree problems constructed by approximating the original problem around the trajectory trees $(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}, \bar{\boldsymbol{u}})$ obtained from the previous iteration:
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align} 
    \min_{\delta \boldsymbol{x}, \delta \boldsymbol{u}} &\sum_{i \in \mathcal{L}} w_i \left( q_i^\top \delta x_i + \frac{1}{2} \delta x_i^\top Q_i \delta x_i  \right) + \nonumber \\  
    &\sum_{i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \mathcal{L}} w_i 
    \left(\begin{bmatrix}
        q_{i} \\  r_{i} 
    \end{bmatrix} ^\top 
    \begin{bmatrix} \delta x_i \\ \delta u_i \end{bmatrix} + \frac{1}{2}
    \begin{bmatrix} \delta x_i \\ \delta u_i \end{bmatrix}^\top 
    \begin{bmatrix}
        Q_{i} & M_{i}^\top\\ M_{i} & R_{i}
    \end{bmatrix} 
    \begin{bmatrix} \delta x_i \\ \delta u_i \end{bmatrix}\right) \label{eq:approx_cost} \\
    \text{s.t.}\quad
    & \delta x_{i} = A_{\text{p}(i)} \delta x_{\text{p}(i)} + B_{\text{p}(i)} \delta u_{\text{p}(i)} + c_{\text{p}(i)},  \forall i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \{0\}, 
\end{align} \label{eq:lqr_tree_approx}
\end{subequations}
where $\delta x_i$ and $\delta u_i$ represent the deviations from the nominal trajectory trees.
The matrices $A_i = \frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x_i}$ and $B_i = \frac{\partial f_i}{\partial u_i}$ describe the linearized system dynamics, while $c_{\text{p}(i)} = f_{\text{p}(i)}(\bar{x}_{\text{p}(i)}, \bar{u}_{\text{p}(i)}) - \bar{x}_i$ denotes the dynamics defect.
The cost matrices are the gradients and Hessian matrices of the Lagrangian, evaluated along the nominal trajectory trees:
\begin{gather*}
    Q_i = \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{L}}{\partial x_i^2},\, R_i = \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{L}}{\partial u_i^2},\, M_i = \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{L}}{\partial u_i \partial x_i},\,
    q_i = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial x_i},\, r_i = \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial u_i},
\end{gather*}
% where $\mathcal{L}: \mathbb{R}^{n_x} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_u} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_x}$ is the Lagrangian expressed as:
where $\mathcal{L}$ is the Lagrangian function expressed as:
\begin{multline*}
    \mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}, \boldsymbol{\lambda}) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{L}} w_i \ell_{f, i}\left(x_i\right) + \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \mathcal{L}} w_i \ell_i\left(x_i, u_i \right) \\
    + \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \{0\}}   \lambda_i^\top \left(x_{i} - f_{\text{p}(i)}(x_{\text{p}(i)}, u_{\text{p}(i)})\right), 
\end{multline*}
with $\lambda_i \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x}$ representing the Lagrange multiplier for the dynamics constraint.
Each LQR-tree problem can be efficiently solved through the Riccati recursion, progressing backward from the leaf nodes to the root, followed by a forward pass to recover the optimal trajectory trees.  
To enhance computational efficiency, we employ the so-called parallel scan to enable temporal parallelism in both the backward and forward passes, as detailed in the following sections.

\subsection{Backward Pass} \label{subsec:backward}
We split the backward pass into two segments, $\mathcal{P}_1$ and $\mathcal{P}_2$, according to their levels of parallelism, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:backward}.
Let $N_b$ denote the last branching time step. 
$\mathcal{P}_1$ represents the portion of the backward pass beginning from the leaf nodes and proceeding to the nodes at time step $N_b + 1$.
$\mathcal{P}_1$ is highly parallelizable, as the parallel scan can be applied to each branch independently. In other words, the backward passes for each branch can be executed in parallel. 
% Once the value functions at time step $T_b + 1$ are obtained, the remaining segment $\mathcal{P}_2$ are solved by applying the Riccati recursion to the initial portion of the trajectory tree.
% In this process, we parallelize the computation across different branches without achieving temporal parallelism.


Once the value functions at time step $N_b + 1$ are obtained, the remaining segment $\mathcal{P}_2$ can be solved via two approaches introduced in Sections \ref{sec:parallel_lqr} and \ref{sec:parallel_dense_lqr}.
First, we can apply the sequential Riccati recursion to the initial portion of the trajectory tree.
During this process, we parallelize the computation across different branches without achieving temporal parallelism.

Alternatively, we can solve the condensed LQR-Tree problem, similar to \eqref{eq:condensed}, with the Hessian $H_\text{tree}$ and the gradient $h_\text{tree}$ adapted to the tree structure. 
We develop a condensing process that enables parallelism across both time steps and scenarios.
Specifically, we flatten the tree in $\mathcal{P}_2$, obtaining $\vert\mathcal{L}\vert$ tree paths, and independently condense the LQR problem for each tree path, producing $H^i$ and $h^i$.
We then define $\delta \boldsymbol u^i := (\delta u^i_k)_{k \in [0, N_b]}$ to represent the input sequence for $i$-th tree path, while $\delta \bar{\boldsymbol u} := (\delta \boldsymbol u^i)_{i \in [1, \vert\mathcal{L}\vert]}$ denotes the stacked input sequence. 
The relation between the input tree $\delta \boldsymbol u$ and the stacked input sequence $\delta \bar{\boldsymbol u}$ is expressed as
$
    \delta \boldsymbol u = \Gamma \delta \bar{\boldsymbol u},
$
where $\Gamma$ is used to eliminate redundant variables associated with shared branches in $\delta \bar{\boldsymbol u}$.
Using the Hessian matrices $H^i$ and the gradient vectors $h^i$ for each tree path $i$, we finally derive $H_\text{tree}$ and $h_\text{tree}$ as follows:
\begin{align}
    H_\text{tree} &= \Gamma^\top  \mathrm{diag}\left(\left[ H^i \right]_{i \in [1, \vert\mathcal{L}\vert]}\right) \Gamma \nonumber \\ 
    h_\text{tree} &= \Gamma^\top \mathrm{diag}\left(\left[ h^i \right]_{i \in [1, \vert\mathcal{L}\vert]}\right),
\end{align}
where $\mathrm{diag}(\,)$ is a function that creates a block diagonal matrix from provided matrices. 

\subsection{Forward Pass}
In an iLQR-based solver, a nonlinear rollout is commonly used \cite{HowellALTRO2019, TassaiLQR2012}.
However, running a nonlinear rollout on GPUs is time-consuming since it cannot be parallelized easily (see Section \ref{sec:results}).  
To optimize computational efficiency, we employ the parallel linear rollout (see Section \ref{subsubsec:par_lin_rollout}) to compute the optimal perturbations of the state and input trees, $(\delta \boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}, \delta \boldsymbol{u}^{l, k})$, where the superscript $k$ is the iteration index.
Our method is classified as a multi-shooting (MS) approach due to the use of the linear rollout.
After the rollout, we execute a line search to determine the best step size $\alpha \in (0, 1]$. 
As the linear rollout cannot guarantee the feasibility of the dynamics, we introduce a $L_1$ merit function to balance cost reduction and constraint violation \cite{LiUnified2023} \cite[Section 18]{Nocedal_numerical_2006}:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:merit}
    M(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}; \mu) = J(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}) + \mu \lVert \boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}) \rVert_1,
\end{equation}
where $J(\cdot,\cdot)$ is the objective function of \eqref{eq:bmpc},
$\mu > 0$ is a penalty weight and $\boldsymbol{d}(\cdot, \cdot)$ is a vector containing all the dynamics defects. 
We decide if the trial step $(\alpha \delta \boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}, \alpha \delta \boldsymbol{u}^{l, k})$ can be accepted based on the following sufficient decrease condition:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:sufficient_decrease}
    M(\boldsymbol{x}^\prime, \boldsymbol{u}^\prime; \mu) \leq M(\boldsymbol{x}^k, \boldsymbol{u}^k; \mu) + 
    \underbrace{
    \beta \left(\text{EC}(\alpha) - \alpha\mu \lVert \boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{x}^k, \boldsymbol{u}^k) \rVert_1 \right)}_{\Delta M}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{x}^\prime = \boldsymbol{x}^k + \alpha\delta\boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}$, $\boldsymbol{u}^\prime = \boldsymbol{u}^k + \alpha\delta\boldsymbol{u}^{l, k}$, and $\beta \in (0, 1)$ is a tuning parameter.
EC($\alpha$) represents the expected cost change, which is calculated by substituting $(\delta \boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}, \delta \boldsymbol{u}^{l, k})$ into \eqref{eq:approx_cost}.
Following \cite{LiUnified2023}, we adopt an adaptive update method for $\mu$ to ensure that the reduction $\text{EC}(\alpha) - \alpha\mu \lVert \boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{x}^k, \boldsymbol{u}^k) \rVert_1$ is sufficient. 
The corresponding update rule is given by:
\begin{align}
\mu^k_{\text{trial}} &= 
\begin{cases}
    \frac{\text{EC}(1)}{(1 - \gamma) \lVert \boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{x}^k, \boldsymbol{u}^k) \rVert_1} + \mu_0 \quad &\text{if}\; \lVert \boldsymbol{d}(\boldsymbol{x}^k, \boldsymbol{u}^k) \rVert_1 > \epsilon, \\
    \mu^{k-1} &\text{otherwise},
\end{cases} \label{eq:mu_update1} \\
\mu^k &= \max(\mu^k_{\text{trial}}, \mu^{k-1}) \label{eq:mu_update2}, 
\end{align}
where $\epsilon > 0$ is a small number, $\mu_0 > 0$ sets a safety margin, and $\gamma \in (0, 1)$ is a tuning parameter.
The computation of trial steps is performed in parallel. 
We then find all feasible step sizes and select the largest one following the backtracking approach in \cite{Nocedal_numerical_2006}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Parallel MSiLQR-Tree}\label{alg:alg1}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\While{stopping criterion not satisfied}
    \State Approximate the original problem around $(\bar{\boldsymbol{x}}, \bar{\boldsymbol{u}})$ to obtain \eqref{eq:lqr_tree_approx}
    \State Initialize the input array using \eqref{eq:back_init}
    \State Apply the parallel scan to solve $\mathcal{P}_1$
    \State Solve $\mathcal{P}_2$ via Riccati recursion or condensing
    \State Compute $(\delta \boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}, \delta \boldsymbol{u}^{l, k})$ via the linear rollout 
    \State Compute $\mu^k$ using \eqref{eq:mu_update1} and \eqref{eq:mu_update2}
    \ParFor{all $\alpha_i$}
        \State $\boldsymbol{x}^\prime \gets \boldsymbol{x}^k + \alpha_i\delta\boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}$
        \State $\boldsymbol{u}^\prime \gets \boldsymbol{u}^k + \alpha_i\delta\boldsymbol{u}^{l, k}$
        \State Evaluate the merit function $ M_i(\boldsymbol{x}^\prime, \boldsymbol{u}^\prime; \mu^k)$ \eqref{eq:merit}
        \State Compute the expected reduction $\Delta M_i$ in \eqref{eq:sufficient_decrease}
    \EndParFor 
    \State Find $\{\alpha_i^\prime\} \subseteq \{\alpha_i\}$ that satisfies 
    \eqref{eq:sufficient_decrease}
    \If{$\{\alpha_i^\prime\} \neq \emptyset$}
        \State $\alpha^* \gets \max\; \{\alpha_i^\prime\}$
    \Else{}
        % \State Increase $\rho$, 
        \State Regularize Hessian matrix
    \EndIf
    \State $\boldsymbol{x}^{k+1} \gets \boldsymbol{x}^k + 
    \alpha^*\delta\boldsymbol{x}^{l, k}$
    \State $\boldsymbol{u}^{k+1} \gets \boldsymbol{u}^k + \alpha^*\delta\boldsymbol{u}^{l, k}$
    \State $k \gets k+1$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\label{alg1}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Augmented Lagrangian (AL) MSiLQR-Tree}
We handle the stage-wise inequality constraints in \eqref{eq:bmpc} via the augmented Lagrangian relaxation \cite{HowellALTRO2019}. The augmented Lagrangian function associated with inequality constraints is given by:
\begin{align} \label{eq:AL}
    &\mathcal{L}_a(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}; \boldsymbol{\eta}_e, \rho) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{L}} w_i \ell_{f, i}\left(x_i\right) + \left(\eta_{e,i} + \frac{1}{2} I_{\rho,i} g_i(x_i) \right)^\top g_i(x_i) \nonumber \\
    &\; + \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \mathcal{L}} w_i \ell_i\left(x_i, u_i \right) + \left(\eta_{e,i} + \frac{1}{2} I_{\rho,i} g_i(x_i, u_i) \right)^\top g_i(x_i, u_i),
\end{align}
where $\eta_{e,i}$ denotes the estimated Lagrange multiplier, and $\rho > 0$ is the penalty weight. 
$I_{\rho, i}$ is a diagonal matrix that selects the active constraints, with the following diagonal elements:
\begin{align*}
    I_{\rho, i, mm} = \begin{cases}
    0 \quad \text{if}\; g_{i, m}(x_i, u_i) < 0\; \text{and}\;  \eta_{e, i, m} = 0\\
    \rho \quad \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $m$ is the index of the $m$-th constraint. 
The relaxed optimal control problem for \eqref{eq:bmpc} is then formulated as
\begin{equation} \label{eq:relax_bmpc}
\left \{ \;\,
\begin{aligned} 
    \min_{\boldsymbol{u}} &\; \mathcal{L}_a(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{u}; \boldsymbol{\eta}_e, \rho) \\
    \text{s.t.}\; & x_0 = \bar{x},\; \\
    & x_{i} = f_{\text{p}(i)}(x_{\text{p}(i)}, u_{\text{p}(i)}),\quad  \forall i \in \mathcal{N} \backslash \{0\}.
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
The AL MSiLQR-Tree method utilizes a double-loop structure. 
The penalty weight and Lagrange multipliers are updated in the outer loop to reinforce constraint satisfaction.
In the inner loop, the relaxed iLQR-Tree problem \eqref{eq:relax_bmpc} is solved using the current values of the penalty weight and Lagrange multipliers.
To ensure that the approximated Hessian for \eqref{eq:relax_bmpc} is positive semi-definite \cite{schraudolph_fast_2002}, we use the generalized Gauss-Newton Hessian approximation due to its ease of computation and widespread application \cite{HowellALTRO2019, GrandiaLocomotion2023}. 

\section{Numerical Simulations} \label{sec:results}
\subsection{Setup}
In this section, we benchmark the proposed algorithms against other iLQR solvers through two case studies.
The trajectory optimization solvers differ in their backward and forward passes.
For the backward pass, we evaluate the proposed parallel backward pass (PBP) and condensed backward pass (CBP), alongside the sequential backward pass (SBP), which uses the standard Riccati recursion. 
Additionally, we differentiate the computing platforms, GPU and CPU, resulting in distinct BP methods: PBP-GPU, CBP-GPU, SBP-GPU, and SBP-CPU.
While SBP-GPU leverages parallelism across scenarios, SBP-CPU is a purely sequential method.
Regarding the forward pass, we explore different rollout methods, including nonlinear rollout (NR) and linear rollout (LR), as described in \cite{LiUnified2023}.
In addition, we consider both parallel line search (PLS) and sequential line search (SLS) techniques. 
The iLQR solvers used for comparison are the following:
\begin{itemize}
    \item PMSiLQR (PBP-{$\mathcal{P}_1$} + SBP-{$\mathcal{P}_2$} + LR + PLS), our proposed method, comprising a parallel backward pass for $\mathcal{P}_1$, a sequential backward pass for $\mathcal{P}_2$ and a parallel line search;
    \item HyPMSiLQR (PBP-{$\mathcal{P}_1$} + CBP-{$\mathcal{P}_2$} + LR + PLS), our proposed hybrid method, comprising a parallel backward pass for $\mathcal{P}_1$, a condensed backward pass for $\mathcal{P}_2$ and a parallel line search;
    \item SMSiLQR (SBP + LR + SLS), a trajectory tree extension of the multiple-shooting iLQR solver presented in \cite{LiUnified2023};
    \item SSSiLQR (SBP + NR + SLS), a classic sequential single shooting iLQR method, implemented in \textsc{Trajax} \cite{trajax}.
\end{itemize}
For notational simplicity, we omit the keyword ``Tree" when naming the methods in this section. 
We implement all algorithms using \textsc{JAX} \cite{jax2018github}.
All simulations are conducted on a laptop with a \SI{2.30}{\giga\hertz} Intel Core i7-11800H processor, NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3060 Laptop, and \SI{16}{\giga\byte} RAM.
The code will be publicly available on GitHub.

