\subsection{Numerical Results} 
In this section, we consider a road vehicle modeled as a unicycle \cite{ChenBranchMPC2022} with four states corresponding to the 2D position, heading angle, and longitudinal velocity. 
The control inputs are the longitudinal acceleration and the yaw rate.

\subsubsection{Impact of horizon length and leaf node count}
This numerical study focuses on an unsignalized intersection-crossing scenario, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:av_scen}. 
In this scenario, the ego vehicle (in blue) intends to turn left, while two surrounding vehicles travel straight. 
% At each decision stage, each surrounding vehicle can adopt one of two behavioral modes: ``Yield'' and ``Assert''.
% To explore different trajectory tree structures, we consider both one-stage and two-stage decision processes for each surrounding vehicle.
% In the one-stage decision process, the vehicle maintains the same behavioral mode throughout the planning horizon, whereas it can switch modes once in the two-stage decision process.
% The corresponding tree structures for different decision processes are shown in Figure \ref{fig:tree_structures}.
We consider a one-stage decision process for each surrounding vehicle, with the corresponding tree structure shown in Figure \ref{fig:tree_structures}.
In this process, each vehicle maintains the same action throughout the planning horizon. 
The action can be a longitudinal velocity target selected from a predefined finite set, denoted by $\mathcal{V}^i$, where $| \mathcal{V}^i |$ represents the number of options available.
The superscript $i \in \{1, 2\}$ indicates the index of the surrounding vehicle.  
By varying $\mathcal{V}^i$ for both vehicles, we can modify the size of the trajectory tree.
For example, choosing $(|\mathcal{V}^1| = 1, |\mathcal{V}^2| = 2)$ results in two leaf nodes. 
We explore five cases involving trajectory trees with 2, 4, 6, 9, and 12 leaf nodes.
In addition, we explore the impact of various planning horizons, $N \in \{63, 127, 255, 511\}$, while keeping the total prediction time and shared prediction time 
fixed at $T = \SI{10}{\second}$ and $T_{\text{sh}} = \SI{0.1}{\second}$, respectively. 

We evaluate the computational performance of the backward and forward passes across various iLQR solvers. 
Figure \ref{fig:BP_time} illustrates the solve times for different BP methods.
Notably, Figure \ref{fig:BP_time_horizon} highlights the substantial computational cost of SBP-GPU, motivating the need for parallel algorithms on GPUs. 
The proposed parallel method, PBP-GPU, outperforms SBP-CPU for longer horizon lengths.
In Figure \ref{fig:BP_time_num_leaf_nodes}, we observe that GPU-based methods maintain nearly constant computational times across a range of leaf node counts, attributed to the parallelism capabilities of GPUs.

The rollout is the most time-consuming operation in the forward pass, requiring multiple executions. 
Figure \ref{fig:FB_time} illustrates the computational performance of different FB methods.
As shown in Figure \ref{fig:FB_time_horizon}, GPU-based methods, with the exception of LR-PLS-GPU, face challenges in executing the rollout. 
Due to the use of the parallel linear rollout method,  LR-PLS-GPU achieves superior performance on GPUs and is less affected by longer horizons.
In contrast, CPU-based methods have an advantage for short-horizon problems.
In addition, methods using a parallel line search scheme (NR-PLS-GPU/NR-PLS-CPU) slightly outperform those with a sequential line search scheme (NR-SLS-GPU/NR-SLS-CPU), as multiple rollouts are executed concurrently.
Similar to the results in the backward pass, Figure \ref{fig:FB_time_num_leaf_nodes} shows that the proposed method, LR-PLS-GPU, is capable of handling a trajectory tree with more tree paths.
Furthermore, Figure \ref{fig:LQR_time} presents the total solve times alongside those for individual components of a single LQR problem.
We observe that the backward pass is the most computationally intensive step.
PMSiLQR achieves approximately $\times 2.5$ and $\times 4.5$ performance improvements in cases with a horizon length of 511 and 12 leaf nodes, respectively.

