\subsubsection{Long shared horizon}
The second case study is presented in \cite{sinha_real-time_2024} and illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:fast_slow}.
The shared branches of a trajectory tree are employed to account for the decision latency inherent in large language models (LLMs).  
Specifically, a fast system can quickly detect anomalies and determine whether to proceed with the current decision or query the slower LLM after $T_{\text{sh}, 0}$. 
After $T_{\text{sh}, 1}$, the LLM makes a final decision, such as continuing with the current decision or adopting a safety backup strategy.
In this study, we set $T_{\text{sh},0} = \SI{0.05}{\second}, T = \SI{5}{\second}$ and $ N = 255$, and explore the influence of varying $T_{\text{sh}, 1}$ values: $\SI{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}{\second}$. 

As introduced in Section \ref{subsec:backward}, $\mathcal{P}_2$ in the backward pass of PMSiLQR is processed sequentially on GPUs.
However, Figure \ref{fig:BP_time} suggests that SBP exhibits poor performance on GPUs, even for short horizons.
Therefore, SBP-GPU is likely to hinder the overall performance of PMSiLQR in problems with long shared horizons.
To overcome this challenge, we instead solve the condensed problem for $\mathcal{P}_2$.
We compare the backward passes of HyPMSiLQR-GPU, PMSiLQR-GPU, and SMSiLQR-CPU. 
Figure \ref{fig:shared_horizon_P2} shows that CBP-GPU significantly outperforms SBP-GPU and achieves performance on par with SBP-CPU when considering short shared horizons (i.e. those less than 75). 
The overall performance is demonstrated in Figure \ref{fig:shared_horizon_BP}. 
Due to the application of the parallel scan to $P_1$, HyPMSiLQR achieves the shortest solve times for the backward pass when dealing with short shared horizons.
However, as the shared horizon increases, sequential methods on CPUs become more advantageous than those on GPUs, as our proposed methods fail to fully exploit the temporal parallel structure in $\mathcal{P}_2$. 

