%\subsubsection{Page-frame migration} 
%\label{page_frame_migrate} 

\textbf{Page Remapping.} It involves OS for page table update and the memory network for page migration to reflect a page remapping, where the virtual page is mapped to a new physical frame belonging to a memory cube suggested by the agent.
We provide \textit{blocking} and \textit{non-blocking} modes for pages with \textit{read-write} and \textit{read-only} permissions, respectively.
For blocking migration, the page is locked during migration and no access is allowed in order to maintain coherence.
For non-blocking migration, the old page frame can be accessed during the migration to reduce performance overhead.
When a page migration is requested by the data remapping decision from the agent, the page number and the new host cube are put into the migration queue of the migration management system.
When the migration DMA can process a new request, the OS is consulted to provide a frame belonging to the new host cube.
Then DMA starts generating migration requests that request data from the old frame and transfer it to the new frame in the new host cube.
Once migration finishes, a migration acknowledgement is sent from the new host to the migration management system, which then reports the migration latency to the memory controller.
Meanwhile, an interrupt is issued to invoke the OS for page table update.
In case of blocking migration, the page is unlocked for accessing.
For non-blocking migration, the old frame is put back to the free frame pool when the outstanding accesses all finish while new accesses use the new mapping.

%the page is term blocking comes from the question whether the page under migration should be blocked for the process to access. The naïve approach is to lock the page in the page-table and invalidate the frame first, so that the memory controller does not issue memory access commands for that page before the transfer completes to ensure correctness. This may block the process hundreds of cycles before it can resume its operations if the consecutive operations are dependent on that page in some way. However, in this way the old frame can be released as soon as the data in that page is transferred to the DMA buffer, that may allow other processes to acquire that free frame. The non-blocking approach allows the process to access the page while migration is going on, by keeping the old frame and also allocating a new frame for the same page. This technique definitely does not block the process as long as frames are abundantly available.  

%Actively accessed pages are periodically chosen as candidates and sent to be scrutinized to the RL agent on their overall fitness in the current location under the current system environment. If the action provided by the agent is for page-migration, it is coupled with the migration candidate and stored in a FIFO queue that belongs to Migration Management System (MMS). MMS is responsible to manage both types of migrations in this system and assisted by a small DMA module (MDMA), separate from the system DMA, only to carry out the migrations. MDMA supports several communication channels to facilitate migration from any node to any other node simultaneously. On each cycle, MMS checks if MDMA can pull a new request from the candidate queue. The source and destination ports must be unoccupied (means no active migration is going on through that port) for MDMA to pickup a candidate and move the frame content into MDMA buffer quickly freeing the node NI. Once the data is available to the MDMA, packetization happens and MDMA administer the injection of those packets back to the network through MDMA-NI to the router following memory network flow control. The MDMA port \ds{is} marked busy when it picks the candidate and marked available once the tail packet is sent to the router. \ds{The} Whole page is sent as one message to minimize the packet header overhead. 

%On the destination side row, 
%\ds{the} activation command is sent while candidate frame is being processed on the source side. The co-ordination is also established by the MDMA for \ds{*scheduled? *scheduling?} schedule memory access commands for both source and destination memory cubes. The commands are issued for read from the source and write into the destination. Since the page reaches the destination node packet by packet, it is buffered until the tail packet \ds{*arrives} is reached, and then written back to the memory in one go following regular frame update procedure. Once the whole migration process is done, an acknowledgement is sent to the OS to notify the event completion and initiate further necessary actions.      

%\subsubsection{Computation Migration}
%\label{compute_migrate} 

\textbf{Computation Remapping.} Computation remapping decouples computation location and the data location for balancing load and improving throughput of the NMP memory network.
%The is implemented by providing an interface with NMP-op scheduler.
Computation cube of an NMP-op is determined by the NMP-op scheduler based on the data address.
The computation cube is then embedded in the offloaded NMP-op request packet.
A compute remap table is used to remap the computation to a different cube suggested by the agent.
When a compute remapping decision related to a page is given by the agent, the page number and the suggestion are stored in the compute remap table.
Upon scheduling an NMP-op, the NMP-op scheduler consults the compute remap table.
If the related page of the NMP-op has an entry in the table, the computation cube is decided based on the agent suggestion recorded in the entry.
Otherwise, the default scheduling is used.
%We provide an interface to indicate (RL action) that the NMP-ops related to a particular page need to be computed in the neighbor of the current location. This information is stored in the NMP-op request packets. The NMP-op manager on the memory cube side orchestrate the necessary data requests to the respective destinations and makes entry of the request in the NMP-op table. The NMP-op is computed once the sources are available using the Processing Units (PEs) available for the NMP-op table.  We provide number of PEs equal to the number of pim-table entry. Once the computation is done, unlike regular computation, the result must be sent to the original memory cube, where the destination page-frame is physically located. This way improves the computation parallelism of the  NMP-ops and also has potential to reduce link energy consumption. 


