\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-0.5em}


This paper presented a multi-agent world model motivated by the conceptual similarity between the progressive denoising process and the incremental reduction of uncertainty in predicting the global next state in MARL. Then, we propose DIMA that models multi-agent dynamics from a centralized perspective while achieving reduced complexity, seamlessly aligning the world model with the underlying MDPs to obtain more accurate predictions. To validate the efficacy of DIMA, we integrated it into the learning-in-imagination training scheme and conducted extensive experiments on the MAMuJoCo and Bi-DexHands benchmarks. The results demonstrated DIMA's superior accuracy and robustness in predicting environment dynamics, as well as its ability to enhance sample efficiency and overall performance. Despite its effectiveness, DIMA may encounter scalability challenges when applied to large-scale multi-agent systems with hundreds of agents. To address this, we plan to explore grouping techniques to further extend DIMA’s applicability and scalability in future work.

% In this paper, we introduced a multi-agent world model inspired by diffusion processes. We were motivated by the conceptual similarity between the progressive denoising process in diffusion models and the incremental reduction of uncertainty in predicting the global next state in MARL, as new agent information is revealed. Building on this insight, we proposed DIMA, a novel multi-agent world model that models multi-agent dynamics from a centralized perspective while achieving reduced complexity. This approach seamlessly aligns the world model with the underlying MDPs, enabling more accurate predictions. To validate the efficacy of DIMA, we integrated it into the learning-in-imagination training scheme and conducted extensive experiments on the MAMuJoCo and Bi-DexHands benchmarks. The results demonstrated DIMA’s superior accuracy and robustness in predicting environment dynamics, as well as its ability to enhance sample efficiency and overall performance. With this work, we promote a more principled approach to modeling multi-agent dynamics by leveraging the unique characteristics of such systems. Despite its effectiveness, DIMA may encounter scalability challenges when applied to large-scale multi-agent systems with hundreds of agents. To address this, we plan to explore grouping techniques to further extend DIMA’s applicability and scalability in future work.

